[{"id": "f358e693-ed99-45ad-ab12-a519b9e53a1c", "episode_id": "18b72ad2-a704-465a-a508-3c4a813cc45d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487d", "chunk_index": 19, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:43:18): Is it the same set of triggers that you would use to induce flow for a group like that group of five in a room try to solve a problem? Can they all respond to the same set of triggers or is it different for everyone?\n\nJeremy Jensen (00:43:32): Yes. So one more distinction to make. The last part, you said they're different for everyone. Lemme talk about that in just one second. The first part of your question is there are some that are the same and some that are distinct. So for instance, yes, and you could certainly do that with yourself. I suppose you could sort of internal monologue be like blah, blah, blah, blah, yes. And then counteract that, but obviously that's more effective in a group setting. So that's a good example. But clear goals is a very good example that is in both an individual and a group trigger. So in fact, and I want to make one thing clear too, I'm sorry. Group flow is largely the most potent phenomenon known to our species. Probably the most meaningful moments we will have as human beings will be in a group flow environment. (00:44:30): Again, you can say maybe it's a music concert, maybe it's lovemaking, maybe it's a deep conversation with someone you care about or that you're deeply interested in. Those tend to be more impactful on average than individual. So it's a very powerful thing. And if you think about it, this is what drives all the forces on the planet, whether it's religion, political, socioeconomics, whatever. People come together and they bond and they share worldview. That tends to be a bonding thing and it drives flow, but that's a soapbox. But anyway, what I wanted to get back to was this idea that you mentioned around, is it the same for everybody? So the answer is, is that Steve always likes to say, and this a popular sort of idea out in this space, is that biology scales personality does not scale. So what we mean by that is that biology is the triggers. (00:45:36): For instance, the 22 triggers that drive flow, that's the same for everybody. It doesn't matter who you are. These activities, risk, novelty, clear goals, challenge skills, balance, whatever, drive flow. But what's different, and what I mean by personality doesn't scale is Cole, what gets you into flow does not get me into flow. So using that spreadsheet example, again, you could be that person that you just love nerding out on the data or getting into a spreadsheet. I hate it. That's the last thing I want to do. So that's an important distinction to make as well, is that everyone has their own thing. Someone could be really into knitting and that gets them off, but I like a mountain bike, just depends.", "token_estimate": 670, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Jeremy Jensen"], "start_timestamp": "00:43:18", "end_timestamp": "00:43:32", "podcast_index": 83, "episode_name": "Ep 83: Jeremy Jensen | Using Flow State to Improve Creativity and Productivity | Crux Academy", "slug": "ep-83-jeremy-jensen-using-flow-state-to-improve-creativity-and-productivity-crux-academy", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-83-jeremy-jensen-using-flow-state-to-improve-creativity-and-productivity-crux-academy", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In 2021 the most valuable commodity is attention. As marketers, we all seek to earn the attention of our audience. But how often do we consider our own focus while on the job? Are those trips to the water cooler really helping your creativity? Does keeping your email tab open or closed help or hinder communication?", "podcast_length": "44:105"}, {"id": "a11ddc60-9a9e-4ed7-96e0-4da746ce9984", "episode_id": "18b72ad2-a704-465a-a508-3c4a813cc45d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487d", "chunk_index": 20, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:46:24): Gotcha. So if I'm a creative director or a marketing director and I'm listening to this and I'm like, wow, imagine what our team could do or solve or get done, if we could encourage some of a little bit of flow into our organization or tap into it, what are some specific examples of things that they could start considering? You've mentioned the deadline challenge is oftentimes a trigger. What are some other kind of examples that could be implemented into an office environment?", "token_estimate": 127, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:46:24", "end_timestamp": "00:46:24", "podcast_index": 83, "episode_name": "Ep 83: Jeremy Jensen | Using Flow State to Improve Creativity and Productivity | Crux Academy", "slug": "ep-83-jeremy-jensen-using-flow-state-to-improve-creativity-and-productivity-crux-academy", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-83-jeremy-jensen-using-flow-state-to-improve-creativity-and-productivity-crux-academy", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In 2021 the most valuable commodity is attention. As marketers, we all seek to earn the attention of our audience. But how often do we consider our own focus while on the job? Are those trips to the water cooler really helping your creativity? Does keeping your email tab open or closed help or hinder communication?", "podcast_length": "44:105"}, {"id": "560a01f0-f78f-4cf1-b049-7b7242d4a0b8", "episode_id": "18b72ad2-a704-465a-a508-3c4a813cc45d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487d", "chunk_index": 21, "content": "Jeremy Jensen (00:46:59): Great question, and I would really want to make sure we talk about something that's super relevant to your audience. So yeah, let's say you're a creative director at a marketing agency or whatever, the first thing you can do is just kind of educate yourself. So there's a lot of resources out there. There's some great books, and I mentioned those. I'm happy to provide some call that you can put in the show notes or whatever. I think, I guess I'll plug Steven Kotler. I mean, the reason why I would is because he's largely the sort of world's foremost thought leader on this topic. Prolific writer has multiple books on the topic. If you're into action sports, I would definitely check out the rise of Superman, even as a marketing executive or a marketing person, you get a lot from that book. It just so happens to use action sports as a case study. (00:47:45): But there's another book he wrote recently called The Art of Impossible. It's all about how you can apply a lot of these concepts at the organizational level. There's a lot of great authors out there. There's some great YouTube videos. I'm happy to provide some of those links, but for a couple tangible things, educate yourself on it, realize it's potential, especially if you're leading a team, we'll look at it for your own self. Hopefully this conversation has got the wheel spinning a little bit of how you can think about structuring your time throughout the day to set yourself up, to minimize distraction, to set clear goals for yourself, starting even the night before. That's a really tried and true technique is like, okay, well tomorrow I want to get this X, Y, Z thing done. Let's nail that down the night before. Let's create a work environment, a space where I can be distraction free. (00:48:40): Let's set time on the calendar to do it. Those types of things can be really effective. So understand the personal implications, but also if you're a team leader, what are the implications for your team? And probably starting with getting a sense of how do your team get their jollies, I guess is the best way to say what gets your team in flow. Understanding that at an individual level, asking them what brings them alive, what tasks do they like? Let's do a check-in. How are you feeling? Do you feel like you experience flow at work? If not, let's try to figure out an environment and a task orientation to where you can do that and then leverage it on the team level. So again, the ideation idea is a great example. If you're trying to solve a problem, if you're trying to come up with a new marketing strategy or campaign, great way to drive new ideas. (00:49:35): I mean, frankly, most good ideas, and frankly, human feats come from being in an optimal state. So I always like to say, when you're making decisions about your life, you don't want to make decisions about your life while you're sitting on the couch eating potato chip. You want to do it when you're feeling amazing, when you're inspired and thinking big, thinking about the potential. So that's one example I would say. But yeah, there's lots of resources out there. If people want to email me, I'm happy to guide you to things play pretty deep in this space, doing some coaching and some advising, but hopefully that's a good start.", "token_estimate": 828, "speakers": ["Jeremy Jensen"], "start_timestamp": "00:46:59", "end_timestamp": "00:46:59", "podcast_index": 83, "episode_name": "Ep 83: Jeremy Jensen | Using Flow State to Improve Creativity and Productivity | Crux Academy", "slug": "ep-83-jeremy-jensen-using-flow-state-to-improve-creativity-and-productivity-crux-academy", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-83-jeremy-jensen-using-flow-state-to-improve-creativity-and-productivity-crux-academy", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In 2021 the most valuable commodity is attention. As marketers, we all seek to earn the attention of our audience. But how often do we consider our own focus while on the job? Are those trips to the water cooler really helping your creativity? Does keeping your email tab open or closed help or hinder communication?", "podcast_length": "44:105"}, {"id": "e58ec8ff-070a-41bc-acc0-a971eb340c8f", "episode_id": "18b72ad2-a704-465a-a508-3c4a813cc45d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487d", "chunk_index": 22, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:50:18): Yeah, wonderful. That creative high that you get every so often, it feels so good, and you're just right. You're just rattling through ideas and you could fill an entire notebook with the amount of ideas that you're coming up with. And then, yeah, it's a slow decline off of that feeling. Are there ways to, if you feel yourself starting to lose the high, can you continue it in ways? Are there things that you can do before it's too late?\n\nJeremy Jensen (00:50:51): Yeah, there are. The curse about this is that the moment you recognize it and that you're cognizant of it, you usually lose it. So that's the cruel irony behind it all. But I would say the best thing to do is just you just practice, right? Just don't let your monkey brain turn on. If you're feeling flow and you're feeling like you, let's say you're in a very creative mode, maybe you're, because you said post-production, you're working on a bunch of deliverables for clients right now, let's use that example. Yeah, yeah. Maybe you want to get this scene just perfect, do the editing or whatever. And you're really in the zone and you're feeling great, but then you think, oh shit, the laundry or my dog, definitely do not act on that. Just like you would in meditation, push it out of your mind, gently allow yourself to come back to the task at hand, and usually you'll drive right back into that state. (00:51:54): So I'd say it's practice, it's it's recognition, and then it being actively harnessing the place you're in. Don't let it go and just let yourself go. The other thing is, I do this with mountain biking a lot, is if I'm on a really fun downhill, sometimes I'm feeling like a million dollars and sometimes I'm just like, ah, for some reason my turns just aren't as this or that, and I get frustrated. And then the moment I'm just like, okay, stop. Just let it be. Let it flow, for lack of a better term, then that's immediately when I drop in and then my performance enhances. I have that buzzy feeling, all of it. So it's a trick. It's a practice, but just let yourself go there, I guess is the best way to say it.", "token_estimate": 532, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Jeremy Jensen"], "start_timestamp": "00:50:18", "end_timestamp": "00:50:51", "podcast_index": 83, "episode_name": "Ep 83: Jeremy Jensen | Using Flow State to Improve Creativity and Productivity | Crux Academy", "slug": "ep-83-jeremy-jensen-using-flow-state-to-improve-creativity-and-productivity-crux-academy", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-83-jeremy-jensen-using-flow-state-to-improve-creativity-and-productivity-crux-academy", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In 2021 the most valuable commodity is attention. As marketers, we all seek to earn the attention of our audience. But how often do we consider our own focus while on the job? Are those trips to the water cooler really helping your creativity? Does keeping your email tab open or closed help or hinder communication?", "podcast_length": "44:105"}, {"id": "e1362336-ac2a-4db4-a209-012a17110e8f", "episode_id": "18b72ad2-a704-465a-a508-3c4a813cc45d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487d", "chunk_index": 23, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:52:42): And using the mountain biking example, I feel like it's when you're in that flow state that you gain the confidence to do that feature that you've never done before, or you reach a point where you're like, you know what? I think I can do it this time. And so you do it and you're like, this is awesome, and you level up. I got to to imagine you could take that exact same feeling and just apply it to work or your career or whatever challenge is sitting in front of you on your computer.", "token_estimate": 129, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:52:42", "end_timestamp": "00:52:42", "podcast_index": 83, "episode_name": "Ep 83: Jeremy Jensen | Using Flow State to Improve Creativity and Productivity | Crux Academy", "slug": "ep-83-jeremy-jensen-using-flow-state-to-improve-creativity-and-productivity-crux-academy", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-83-jeremy-jensen-using-flow-state-to-improve-creativity-and-productivity-crux-academy", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In 2021 the most valuable commodity is attention. As marketers, we all seek to earn the attention of our audience. But how often do we consider our own focus while on the job? Are those trips to the water cooler really helping your creativity? Does keeping your email tab open or closed help or hinder communication?", "podcast_length": "44:105"}, {"id": "74aeabb2-1a50-4fbd-b96b-872f5aaee3fe", "episode_id": "18b72ad2-a704-465a-a508-3c4a813cc45d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487d", "chunk_index": 24, "content": "Jeremy Jensen (00:53:10): Exactly. I love that call. That's exactly right. I want to example there is, there's a mental thought exercise of like, okay, what if you said you have goals for yourself. Let's say you have a goal and do you want to accomplish in the next five years, just as a thought experiment, what if you decided to try to accomplish that same goal in six months? That's a big thesis around this space. And actually that's Steven's deep interest area. It's achieving the impossible. And that's what's interesting about all of this is that I like to use, I happen to be friends with Alex, and I think he's a really good example of what's possible. So I imagine most of your listeners would know who he is, but Klein alcap without a rope a couple of years back and was demonstrating that movie free solo that took so much, there's so many multifaceted layers to that. (00:54:13): There's endurance, there's strength, there's mindset, there's all these things. And so when you think about that, no one has ever, what I love about what he did is most sports at a really high level, let's just take track and field or swimming or even ski basing, whatever, the difference between first and second place is tenths of a second or hundreds of a second. There's no one even thinking about what Alex did. No one's really actually seriously thinking about that. So incredible about what he did, and clearly he was pushing the edge of human performance to the max. So it's a really cool case study, and it speaks to what you're saying is that you can absolutely push the envelope as you should. And so it's usually about a mindset piece. It's what can you actually accomplish? And part of it, of course, is to believe you can do it. (00:55:07): So there's a mindset, growth mindset. Carol Dweck's work on mindset. The book literally called Mindset by Stanford professor Carol Dweck, very good source text. But then there's also the systematization of getting the work done day in day out to reach the goal. So Alex didn't just fuck around to make that happen. He trained like a maniac and he jumped off the first few times. It's the same for us. So if you say, alright, my five-year goal, there's no way I could accomplish that in six months. Are you sure? Literally, absolutely. It applies to the work environment.", "token_estimate": 580, "speakers": ["Jeremy Jensen"], "start_timestamp": "00:53:10", "end_timestamp": "00:53:10", "podcast_index": 83, "episode_name": "Ep 83: Jeremy Jensen | Using Flow State to Improve Creativity and Productivity | Crux Academy", "slug": "ep-83-jeremy-jensen-using-flow-state-to-improve-creativity-and-productivity-crux-academy", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-83-jeremy-jensen-using-flow-state-to-improve-creativity-and-productivity-crux-academy", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In 2021 the most valuable commodity is attention. As marketers, we all seek to earn the attention of our audience. But how often do we consider our own focus while on the job? Are those trips to the water cooler really helping your creativity? Does keeping your email tab open or closed help or hinder communication?", "podcast_length": "44:105"}, {"id": "3fecd05a-d04b-4c92-9d74-ab7c5af7744e", "episode_id": "18b72ad2-a704-465a-a508-3c4a813cc45d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487d", "chunk_index": 25, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:55:53): How has obviously flow has affected your life in a number of ways? You've given some examples in the day to day. How has this affected you? How do you think about projects or how do you think about goals yourself or I got to imagine having this knowledge can be a blessing and a curse because you sometimes are in these great moments and you're being productive, and then other times you're not in flow but so much about flow. You're like, why am I not in flow? I dunno, what's that?\n\nJeremy Jensen (00:56:26): Yeah, I appreciate that. Yeah, that's very true. I would say it's more beneficial to know more about it than not beneficial, although there is some of that going on as I'm doing all the right things, why am I not dropping into flow? But that's rare, I would say. I think it is very well, there's a prescription almost, and it really is the triggers. It's just understanding what to do. So I think for how it's affected me is, well, first of all, it, it's made me so much more productive. It's made me more fulfilled. It's made me more present in my relationships with my wife, my nine month old daughter. She brings me so much joy. Talk about a flow trigger, oh my good Lord, oxytocin through the roof. But yeah, I mean, I think it's just once you start to understand it all, it does kind of come together. Now, I don't want to say that this is the end all be all. It's not, and it's a constant process, and I have a long, long, long way to go. But I think there's been some significant strides, and I think anyone can start from a base level and start to apply some of this stuff to their life and really see really significant results. If nothing else, they're just feeling better day in, day out.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:57:51): You mentioned you have a long ways to go. Where are you trying to go?", "token_estimate": 459, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Jeremy Jensen"], "start_timestamp": "00:55:53", "end_timestamp": "00:57:51", "podcast_index": 83, "episode_name": "Ep 83: Jeremy Jensen | Using Flow State to Improve Creativity and Productivity | Crux Academy", "slug": "ep-83-jeremy-jensen-using-flow-state-to-improve-creativity-and-productivity-crux-academy", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-83-jeremy-jensen-using-flow-state-to-improve-creativity-and-productivity-crux-academy", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In 2021 the most valuable commodity is attention. As marketers, we all seek to earn the attention of our audience. But how often do we consider our own focus while on the job? Are those trips to the water cooler really helping your creativity? Does keeping your email tab open or closed help or hinder communication?", "podcast_length": "44:105"}, {"id": "98cb0134-67b7-4a57-b2b1-ba61fb1533a9", "episode_id": "18b72ad2-a704-465a-a508-3c4a813cc45d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487d", "chunk_index": 26, "content": "Jeremy Jensen (00:57:56): Well, I mean, I'm trying to grow this new company Corrects Academy, and that's my big focus right now. And that's going well. But yeah, I mean, we're just scratching the surface. We've just barely off the ground. And so very optimistic about that. And I have a lot of, I mean, if you want to hear specific goals, I'm happy to tell you. But yeah, I mean have again, my short-term goals, I kind of like to call those high hard goals, like the two three year range, massive transformative purpose. That's your life goal. What on your deathbed, what do you want to be leaving to the world? And I think it's effective to work on those levels. It's like, okay, what's your MTP? How do you break that down into chunks, into literally decades and even years? And then from the year goals, how do you break that down into quarters, months, days? I mean, frankly even hours. And so it kind of sounds daunting, but when you actually break it down that way, it's very motivating. So I certainly have plenty of goals that I'm shooting for.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:59:05): Gotcha. Right on. You mentioned music. I want to make sure we touch on this because it sounds like music is, well, what is music? How does it affect flow?", "token_estimate": 309, "speakers": ["Jeremy Jensen", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:57:56", "end_timestamp": "00:59:05", "podcast_index": 83, "episode_name": "Ep 83: Jeremy Jensen | Using Flow State to Improve Creativity and Productivity | Crux Academy", "slug": "ep-83-jeremy-jensen-using-flow-state-to-improve-creativity-and-productivity-crux-academy", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-83-jeremy-jensen-using-flow-state-to-improve-creativity-and-productivity-crux-academy", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In 2021 the most valuable commodity is attention. As marketers, we all seek to earn the attention of our audience. But how often do we consider our own focus while on the job? Are those trips to the water cooler really helping your creativity? Does keeping your email tab open or closed help or hinder communication?", "podcast_length": "44:105"}, {"id": "f3f9eb1e-76fd-4875-a312-98948eab7a08", "episode_id": "18b72ad2-a704-465a-a508-3c4a813cc45d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487d", "chunk_index": 27, "content": "Jeremy Jensen (00:59:15): Yeah, great question. So I won't nerd out too much on this, but basically we've talked about, there's three relevant areas of the brain that we're talking about when we're talking about flow. So there's neurochemistry, which we talked about a little bit. So there's five flow chemicals, dopamine, norepinephrine, oxytocin, and that was five. Then there's the neuroanatomy. So that's the parts of the brain. We talked about the prefrontal cortex, there's amygdala, those types of things that are fear-based, that type of thing. And then the last part is the neuro electricity. So when it comes to music, that's what we're working on. So it turns out there's flow happens in this alpha theta range. So brainwaves are measured in megahertz and turns out that you want to get into that alpha theta zone so that you can induce flow. Well, if you think about music, the beats of the music and especially repetition can put you into that electric signature. And so that's what I mean by music. And so definitely people, I mean, I think most people listen to music for a reason that how it makes them feel. So most music will get you there. But in particular for doing deep work, if you're trying to be productive, there's some great resources out there to drive you into that particular electric signature. I'm actually trying to create a little, I kind of like SaaS, the SaaS world. (01:01:06): I'm big believer in SaaS products. I'm big into no code and that type of thing. So I'm actually building in all of my spare time a neural beats platform where you can just, it's like a player where you can listen to music that drives flow. But in the meantime, I don't have it launched. So in the meantime, I'll talk about the big competitor in this space because I use it all the time. Brain fm, highly recommend the guy, I think his name's Nathan. I want to say is the founder, he takes a very neuroscience backed approach, and if you go to that site, you can play free music. He drives flow very well, so he always likes to say, if you put headphones on and give it 10 minutes, you're going to be found pretty good.", "token_estimate": 534, "speakers": ["Jeremy Jensen"], "start_timestamp": "00:59:15", "end_timestamp": "00:59:15", "podcast_index": 83, "episode_name": "Ep 83: Jeremy Jensen | Using Flow State to Improve Creativity and Productivity | Crux Academy", "slug": "ep-83-jeremy-jensen-using-flow-state-to-improve-creativity-and-productivity-crux-academy", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-83-jeremy-jensen-using-flow-state-to-improve-creativity-and-productivity-crux-academy", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In 2021 the most valuable commodity is attention. As marketers, we all seek to earn the attention of our audience. But how often do we consider our own focus while on the job? Are those trips to the water cooler really helping your creativity? Does keeping your email tab open or closed help or hinder communication?", "podcast_length": "44:105"}, {"id": "bdc5d3ff-6481-4e40-a6e9-1114bb46142c", "episode_id": "18b72ad2-a704-465a-a508-3c4a813cc45d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487d", "chunk_index": 28, "content": "Cole Heilborn (01:02:00): That's cool. I'm going to have to check that out. That sounds pretty neat. Well, Jeremy, I want to thank you for taking the time to chat. This has been, I feel like we've only just scratched the surface, but we've scratched enough that I want to learn more and I want to get out there and do a little more research on this topic of flow. You've mentioned a bunch of resources throughout the episode, throughout the time today. I think it'd be great if we could collect those and drop those in the show notes, just so folks have 'em handy. But if there was two resources that they should take away, what would you recommend people look into if they're trying to dig deeper into this?\n\nJeremy Jensen (01:02:38): Yeah, I would say get yourself a hold of one of these books. I'd say because of your audience, I think Rise of Superman is a great read. That is just, it's sort of what Steven's earlier books, and I think it's kind of set the stage for a lot of this work. That's one. The other one I would say is Cal Newport, his book called Deep Work, or he also wrote one on digital minimalism. So for the readers out there, grab something like that. Also really worth just Googling or YouTubing Flow, how to get into flow. A lot of people are talking about it at this point, so you'll see some good resources and then I would definitely check out Brain fm. It's just a good resource.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:03:29): Awesome. Jeremy, anything else that you want to leave the audience with? Anything that we didn't touch on or anything we should come back to?", "token_estimate": 394, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Jeremy Jensen"], "start_timestamp": "01:02:00", "end_timestamp": "01:03:29", "podcast_index": 83, "episode_name": "Ep 83: Jeremy Jensen | Using Flow State to Improve Creativity and Productivity | Crux Academy", "slug": "ep-83-jeremy-jensen-using-flow-state-to-improve-creativity-and-productivity-crux-academy", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-83-jeremy-jensen-using-flow-state-to-improve-creativity-and-productivity-crux-academy", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In 2021 the most valuable commodity is attention. As marketers, we all seek to earn the attention of our audience. But how often do we consider our own focus while on the job? Are those trips to the water cooler really helping your creativity? Does keeping your email tab open or closed help or hinder communication?", "podcast_length": "44:105"}, {"id": "194b21fc-9543-4f49-810e-b17888b60757", "episode_id": "18b72ad2-a704-465a-a508-3c4a813cc45d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487d", "chunk_index": 29, "content": "Jeremy Jensen (01:03:36): Oh, so much you got me into flow in this conversation. Sorry, my mind's spinning and there's so much I would love to talk about, but no, hopefully this was useful. I just want to circle back and again, try to be really relevant to your listeners and just note that as marketing professionals, you're in a creative space, so that's a win automatically, especially if you're in the outdoor industry marketing space. Also another win, so definitely if you're in an outdoor marketing role, you're probably really primed or naturally inclined to want to leverage flow more effectively or leverage flow in your life in general. There's probably a reason you gravitated towards marketing and also the outdoor industry, so that's a good sign. So definitely worth looking into On sort of a selfish note, I'm always looking to make contacts with marketing teams in the outdoor space. We do a lot of partnerships in the work I do, so please be in touch if it's of interest, happy to talk about flow till I'm blue in the face and just connect with people, especially in the mountain west and Pacific Northwest. (01:04:50): Yeah, please let me know if I can help at all. I love this stuff. The other thing I'll say is just the outdoor space is just such a exciting place to be in. I don't need to tell your listeners that, but I'm just really excited for where the industry is headed. I wouldn't be doubling down on it if I didn't believe in it. I think there's amazing people in this space that think we have some work to do around diversity, equity, inclusion, accessibility, that type of thing, and I'm working on that for one, but I'm just honored to be in a space with people who have a good balance between work and play and who are just cool fucking people around all the way around. So thank you so much, Cole, for having me on the show.", "token_estimate": 465, "speakers": ["Jeremy Jensen"], "start_timestamp": "01:03:36", "end_timestamp": "01:03:36", "podcast_index": 83, "episode_name": "Ep 83: Jeremy Jensen | Using Flow State to Improve Creativity and Productivity | Crux Academy", "slug": "ep-83-jeremy-jensen-using-flow-state-to-improve-creativity-and-productivity-crux-academy", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-83-jeremy-jensen-using-flow-state-to-improve-creativity-and-productivity-crux-academy", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In 2021 the most valuable commodity is attention. As marketers, we all seek to earn the attention of our audience. But how often do we consider our own focus while on the job? Are those trips to the water cooler really helping your creativity? Does keeping your email tab open or closed help or hinder communication?", "podcast_length": "44:105"}, {"id": "503fb594-ad60-4b2f-969b-fb92bf530886", "episode_id": "18b72ad2-a704-465a-a508-3c4a813cc45d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487d", "chunk_index": 30, "content": "Cole Heilborn (01:05:34): Absolutely. And speaking to everyone who's listening, we'd love to hear your feedback. This is an episode that kind of breaks the mold for some of the other episodes we've put out. We talk a lot about marketing. Specifically. This topic is a little more about how to maybe think about we're doing work or how to consider approaching work, and if this episode resonates and do you feel like we should do more of these, I don't know, leadership kind of process related topics. Shoot us a note. We'd love to hear your notes. Jeremy, though, thank you. If folks want to connect with you, where can they go to find you?\n\nJeremy Jensen (01:06:12): Yeah, Jeremy at Crux Academy is probably the best place to hit me. There's no.com, just Crux Academy. You can also hit me up on Instagram, Jeremy R. Jensen on Twitter as well. Yeah, happy to help wherever I can. Oh, and you made me think of one other thing as a marketer. Remember, this is a good thing to think about as far as attracting consumers and the way to position products. Right? Sorry, one more other thing. Plug is the book Stealing Fire. That book takes a much more macro view of optimal states of consciousness for marketers. I think it's particularly interesting because it looks at how it affects economic forces. So if you think about movies and video games and porn, action, sports and travel, so many industries have flow at the center because it changes our state, entertainment, sports, all those things. So it actually has a lot of relevancy as far as how marketers think about content and maybe marketing channels and that type of thing too. So just want to delay that, plant, that seed.", "token_estimate": 420, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Jeremy Jensen"], "start_timestamp": "01:05:34", "end_timestamp": "01:06:12", "podcast_index": 83, "episode_name": "Ep 83: Jeremy Jensen | Using Flow State to Improve Creativity and Productivity | Crux Academy", "slug": "ep-83-jeremy-jensen-using-flow-state-to-improve-creativity-and-productivity-crux-academy", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-83-jeremy-jensen-using-flow-state-to-improve-creativity-and-productivity-crux-academy", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In 2021 the most valuable commodity is attention. As marketers, we all seek to earn the attention of our audience. But how often do we consider our own focus while on the job? Are those trips to the water cooler really helping your creativity? Does keeping your email tab open or closed help or hinder communication?", "podcast_length": "44:105"}, {"id": "ebd37793-62c2-4412-b5e4-d16c880e578d", "episode_id": "18b72ad2-a704-465a-a508-3c4a813cc45d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487d", "chunk_index": 31, "content": "Cole Heilborn (01:07:30): No, that's great. And I think as marketers, all we're doing is just talking to other people. It comes down to communication and the more we understand about who we are as people and the people we're talking to, and sometimes that's emotions, sometimes that's interest. Other times it's philosophical and it's neurological and understanding what's actually happening in the brain. So I guess that's why I'm really stoked about having this conversation because I feel like it gets us into a whole nother topic that we've never really delved into. So Jeremy, thanks again for your time. Have a great rest of your day and enjoy your Thanksgiving.\n\nJeremy Jensen (01:08:07): Thanks, Cole. Likewise. Super cool to chat.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:08:10): Alright, see you.\n\nJeremy Jensen (01:08:10): See you.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:08:13): Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learned something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend. I.", "token_estimate": 266, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Jeremy Jensen"], "start_timestamp": "01:07:30", "end_timestamp": "01:08:13", "podcast_index": 83, "episode_name": "Ep 83: Jeremy Jensen | Using Flow State to Improve Creativity and Productivity | Crux Academy", "slug": "ep-83-jeremy-jensen-using-flow-state-to-improve-creativity-and-productivity-crux-academy", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-83-jeremy-jensen-using-flow-state-to-improve-creativity-and-productivity-crux-academy", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In 2021 the most valuable commodity is attention. As marketers, we all seek to earn the attention of our audience. But how often do we consider our own focus while on the job? Are those trips to the water cooler really helping your creativity? Does keeping your email tab open or closed help or hinder communication?", "podcast_length": "44:105"}, {"id": "3905c381-99f0-4116-821b-62537789604b", "episode_id": "14be81d4-1448-43e9-aa98-872c817c1655", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487a", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Viktor Marohnic (00:02): On that channel, we have to approach it a little bit differently, like get to convert those users into leads that we can retarget later. So typically what we do, we are trying to do some kind of a lower barrier to entry. So we do this thing that we call webinars with the local guide from Thailand who talks for 40 minutes an hour showing slides and talking about the itinerary of the trip and the details. And people will sign up for that and listen to that. And then later it's much easier for them to convert and say, okay, I want to go with you guys because I love what\n\nCole Heilborn (00:32): I heard. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing podcast. My name is Cole Helebo and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Back Country Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Portside Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Hilborn. Today I'm sitting down with Victor Maron. He's the founder of 57 Hours as well as the founder of the New York based agency Five Victor, how are you?\n\nViktor Marohnic (01:19): I'm doing great. Thanks for having me on your podcast.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:21): Yeah, thanks for joining me today.\n\nViktor Marohnic (01:24): No worries. Yeah, I'm excited to have this conversation. So let's get going.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:30): Yeah, let's do it. Where are you calling in from?\n\nViktor Marohnic (01:33): I mean, I'm based in New York in Williamsburg. It's like a north side of the Brooklyn.", "token_estimate": 462, "speakers": ["Viktor Marohnic", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:02", "end_timestamp": "01:33", "podcast_index": 84, "episode_name": "Ep 84: Viktor Marohnic | User Acquisition and Lowering the Point of Entry | 57 Hours", "slug": "ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "57 Hours is a business that connects customers with local guides for adventures. Viktor Marohnic, the Founder and CEO, joins me on the podcast to discuss the challenges they have faced in streamlining their customer acquisition pipeline.", "podcast_length": "44:106"}, {"id": "5715228e-d011-4739-8f19-c5a6591755a5", "episode_id": "14be81d4-1448-43e9-aa98-872c817c1655", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487a", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Cole Heilborn (01:40): Yeah. Okay. Right on. Well, we've got a great topic for today. We're going to be discussing user acquisition in regards to 57 hours and kind of some of the unique challenges and opportunities that you guys have found through trying to find customers and connect with your customers. I'd love if you could kind of give us a quick introduction on yourself, a little bit of background on the agency that you started as well as 57 hours.\n\nViktor Marohnic (02:07): Yeah, so my background is in computing science. I graduated from computing science college 20 years ago, and then I worked a couple of years as a developer. Then I started my own agency. I had a couple of startups, some successful, some failed. And then when it comes to the topic that we are talking about today, probably sometimes six, seven years ago, working for my agency and doing a bunch of product strategy, product design work, product development work for our clients, we figured out that it's actually a smaller part of what it takes for the product to be successful in the market. And big part comes later once when you build everything you launch. One of the big things that clients were struggling was basically marketing strategy, digital marketing strategy, and especially user acquisition. So sometimes in 2017 18, we launched, we call it growth marketing. (03:18): I like term performance marketing, and we started hiring data scientists and engineers to the marketing part of the work. And we were quite successful. That team grew from two to 35 people in a couple of years and we got a lot of experience in that area. And now I'm applying a bunch of that stuff on 57 hours and still pushing trying new things. So yeah, that's kind of how I got excited about this. And I think it's understanding how digital marketing or performance marketing works is crucial for any product out there on the market today. It used to be I started working in tech in early two thousands, and at that point you were able to launch pretty much anything and you would get initial traction with doing nothing because there was not that many things out there. People were hungry for new things and basically it was enough to launch something, tell your friends, and you would get first batch of users and it was more like build it and they'll come. And these days it's not like you have to figure out your marketing and growth strategy, and that's pretty much what makes or breaks most of the startups these days. I think.", "token_estimate": 626, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Viktor Marohnic"], "start_timestamp": "01:40", "end_timestamp": "02:07", "podcast_index": 84, "episode_name": "Ep 84: Viktor Marohnic | User Acquisition and Lowering the Point of Entry | 57 Hours", "slug": "ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "57 Hours is a business that connects customers with local guides for adventures. Viktor Marohnic, the Founder and CEO, joins me on the podcast to discuss the challenges they have faced in streamlining their customer acquisition pipeline.", "podcast_length": "44:106"}, {"id": "4ba0a5ea-a0e4-4d15-b211-792049909749", "episode_id": "14be81d4-1448-43e9-aa98-872c817c1655", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487a", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Cole Heilborn (04:45): Well, times have certainly changed a little bit as you just alluded to. I'm curious, you made the distinction between performance and growth marketing. Where's that come from? What's the philosophy behind that?\n\nViktor Marohnic (04:58): I mean, we call it growth marketing. Everybody calls it growth marketing and growth marketers. I'm just saying that I like the words he never, it's not as popular, but it's basically the same thing. I like performance marketing, which I think better describes what we actually do because again, most of the people that we hire have some kind of engineering background or some background in data science. And it's all about numbers because you can measure pretty much everything. You can measure it to cents, what's your user acquisition cost, what's your lifetime value? And you can really attribute every dollar spent to every user. And it's all about the numbers there is, we are kind of eliminating, I mean I wouldn't say eliminating, but creativity is not the main driver anymore of successful marketing. It's more like, okay, we'll try anything, whatever, and we'll see what works and whichever idea wins. But it's not like a subjective, no, I like this. I'm excited about this idea. We measure everything and whatever performs better is the winning strategy and winning marketing strategy.\n\nCole Heilborn (06:19): Sure, sure. So tell us a little bit about 57 hours. What is that company?", "token_estimate": 364, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Viktor Marohnic"], "start_timestamp": "04:45", "end_timestamp": "06:19", "podcast_index": 84, "episode_name": "Ep 84: Viktor Marohnic | User Acquisition and Lowering the Point of Entry | 57 Hours", "slug": "ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "57 Hours is a business that connects customers with local guides for adventures. Viktor Marohnic, the Founder and CEO, joins me on the podcast to discuss the challenges they have faced in streamlining their customer acquisition pipeline.", "podcast_length": "44:106"}, {"id": "667c48a7-cad6-4bd3-9189-a0daea4f99f9", "episode_id": "14be81d4-1448-43e9-aa98-872c817c1655", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487a", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Viktor Marohnic (06:24): Yeah, so 57 hours, as I said, I was building digital products and we started as a mobile app development agency. When mobile came out, iPhone didn't even, you couldn't even build apps for iPhone when we started. We were building apps for Nokia and Blackberry phones. And so I was excited about tech and I worked in tech industry since my, I dunno, when I was 16, so 20 something years, 25 years. And then at some point I started thinking about how can I, and I'm also a occupation at Climber and a skier. And when we moved to us from Croatia to us, I started going out with friends and they would hire mountain guides and I was like, okay, this is interesting. I wasn't familiar with the concept, so I got a little bit more into that. And then at some point at the same time I was thinking about how can I bring my patient for tech and my knowhow that I have over there and my patients were outdoors together and build a product in that space. (07:34): And we started a bunch of different ideas from avalanche safety drones to whatever, and we ended up building 57 hours, which is something like Airbnb or Uber for adventures and guides, mountain guides. So the market is extremely fragmented. There is no clear leader or winner in this space. Basically when you are hiring a guide or booking an adventure, you will have to find pretty much every time you're doing it, you'll have to find a new agency in that specific area, book through them. You never know what you're expecting. Booking process is very different every time and a lot of research that you need to invest, where do you want go, who you want to go with. So it's that the industry is in a state where most of the industries were 20, 30 years ago, so it's ready to be upgraded, that kind of user experience. And that was the idea. I mean you can see this happening pretty much with Uber. Airbnb are the most famous. You take existing industry and you just build a superior user experience, customer experience for existing market. And this is what we are doing for 57 hours. And user acquisition is probably the biggest challenge that we have.", "token_estimate": 534, "speakers": ["Viktor Marohnic"], "start_timestamp": "06:24", "end_timestamp": "06:24", "podcast_index": 84, "episode_name": "Ep 84: Viktor Marohnic | User Acquisition and Lowering the Point of Entry | 57 Hours", "slug": "ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "57 Hours is a business that connects customers with local guides for adventures. Viktor Marohnic, the Founder and CEO, joins me on the podcast to discuss the challenges they have faced in streamlining their customer acquisition pipeline.", "podcast_length": "44:106"}, {"id": "f49430e7-f315-4372-9a70-c465a734de87", "episode_id": "14be81d4-1448-43e9-aa98-872c817c1655", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487a", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Cole Heilborn (09:13): And so just to clarify, you guys are a service that operates around the world, connecting customers with local guides for a variety of types of experiences.\n\nViktor Marohnic (09:27): We claim that we have the world biggest database of the guides, so like five, 6,000 guides and growing pretty much all over the world, different sports. It's not just mountain guiding, it's also mountain biking, hiking, sea kayaking, all that stuff. Yeah. And our inventory is growing constantly and we are adding more and more trips. But the goal is that as an outdoor, somebody who is passionate about the outdoors and is looking do something in any part of the world, should be able to find a good reliable partner guide through us.\n\nCole Heilborn (10:15): I see. And where did the name 57 hours come from?\n\nViktor Marohnic (10:18): 57 hours. It's kind of like a weekend warrior thing. It's Friday, 3:00 PM to Sunday midnight, that's 57 hours. And it came from when I used to work as a software developer. My family, I had kids very early and we would go for ski on weekends and climb. And typically what we would do, we would try to figure out how we can get out work a little bit earlier on Friday around 3:00 PM and then we would drive to a ski resort and then ski Saturday, Sunday and then drive back home. So that's 57 hours.\n\nCole Heilborn (10:57): Okay, nice. I love that\n\nViktor Marohnic (10:59): How you maximize your free time. It's kind of 57 hours to help you maximizing the free time that you have available. Instead of you researching and trying to figure out where to go, you just book through 57 hours and you can use your time more efficiently.\n\nCole Heilborn (11:17): Gotcha. And when did you guys start?", "token_estimate": 433, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Viktor Marohnic"], "start_timestamp": "09:13", "end_timestamp": "11:17", "podcast_index": 84, "episode_name": "Ep 84: Viktor Marohnic | User Acquisition and Lowering the Point of Entry | 57 Hours", "slug": "ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "57 Hours is a business that connects customers with local guides for adventures. Viktor Marohnic, the Founder and CEO, joins me on the podcast to discuss the challenges they have faced in streamlining their customer acquisition pipeline.", "podcast_length": "44:106"}, {"id": "ea4b2296-a46f-4c2b-8a54-1a7e485baf16", "episode_id": "14be81d4-1448-43e9-aa98-872c817c1655", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487a", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Viktor Marohnic (11:20): I mean the idea of first idea was born probably four years ago. We spent two years in research doing some prototypes, different how do we want to approach this market? We had hundreds and hundreds of meetings with mountain guides and just trying to understand how they work, how they operate, where they have problems, where are the challenges. And we develop some strong relationship with that community, which is super important these days. But the product was launched last January, the marketplace, and we got some good early traction and then COVID happened and then pretty much everything stopped for six months. So we started basically selling trips again in October last year. And since then we have a pretty strong growth. We are growing almost like 30 to 30% month over month. Actually this month was even more than it, almost 60%. So yeah, it looks like we figured out how to scale this and now we have a really nice trajectory.\n\nCole Heilborn (12:46): Awesome. Well, let's dive into some of the challenges that you guys have faced in finding customers with the service. With what you guys offer. I imagine it's a very specific type of individual at a very specific moment who's looking to book a trip? What are the challenges that you guys have faced finding users, acquiring those customers?", "token_estimate": 333, "speakers": ["Viktor Marohnic", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "11:20", "end_timestamp": "12:46", "podcast_index": 84, "episode_name": "Ep 84: Viktor Marohnic | User Acquisition and Lowering the Point of Entry | 57 Hours", "slug": "ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "57 Hours is a business that connects customers with local guides for adventures. Viktor Marohnic, the Founder and CEO, joins me on the podcast to discuss the challenges they have faced in streamlining their customer acquisition pipeline.", "podcast_length": "44:106"}, {"id": "03b66d35-e9cc-4851-98b0-141e870c4627", "episode_id": "14be81d4-1448-43e9-aa98-872c817c1655", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487a", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Viktor Marohnic (13:11): Google came up with a study years ago about how people go about booking locations and trips and they did a bunch of research because they say a ton of data and they publish that research and it says something that basically they broke down that kind of decision-making phase into actually that process into two phases. One, they call it inspirational phase and the second one they call decision-making phase. So inspirational is something, let's say that you are thinking about going climbing to Thailand, or maybe you saw a YouTube movie or I dunno, an ad. And basically you're now like, okay, this sounds great, we should go one day. But that one day might be next month. But quite often it's few months down the road, sometimes a couple of years down the road. So people are not necessarily making a decision, it caught their attention, they think it's cool, but they're not ready to make a decision right now because it's a big thing. (14:14): You have to commit to travel, it's probably expensive. And then there is a second phase which Google calls decision-making phase or something like that. I'm not sure where people actually, okay, now we want to go to Thailand next summer and now we are looking for guides a trip. What we going to do, we are planning the trip and we kind of focused on that decision making phase, which is basically Google search. We are also now doing something inspirational, but let's cover this first. So here for us, the most important thing is to dominate search. Search pretty much Google search, when people are looking for whatever term climbing in Thailand, we want to be on top of the search results. The problem with that search strategy is that the volume is very limited on pretty much most of the locations. So for example, mountain biking in it's very low volume keyword. (15:14): And for us, in order to grow, we need to win hundreds and hundreds of these keywords. It cannot be just one keyword that we are constantly optimizing for. We need to hundreds of locations, hundreds of different sports, and for each one we have to approach, we have to build our SEO for each one of those locations. So our scaling strategy is basically repeating that success recipe for hundreds and hundreds of locations. So that's pretty much SEO search engine marketing works fine as well. And then we started doing something on that first phase inspirational phase where we are targeting more, it's more like social driven. So we will, because if you run an ad for climbing in Thailand on Facebook, most of the people will see that for the first time and they are kind of like, oh, okay, this looks cool. They might click on an ad, but they're definitely not going to book the trip right away. (16:15): It's kind of really, really rare. So on that channel, we have to approach it a little bit differently. We have to convert those users into leads that we can retarget later. So typically what we do, we are trying to do some kind of a lower barrier to entry. So we do this thing that we call webinars. It's an online event with a local guide from Thailand who talks for 40 minutes an hour showing slides and talking about the itinerary of the trip and the details. And people will sign up for that and listen to that. And then later it's much easier for them to convert and say, okay, I want to go with you guys because I loved what I heard. So we have to have some kind of intermediate conversion point before we can actually convert the customer. So that's something that's kind of new. We are only doing it for last maybe six months or so, and now we're trying to figure out how to scale that. But yeah, it's not like your typical e-commerce where you run an ad on Facebook and people click and buy or don't buy right away.", "token_estimate": 946, "speakers": ["Viktor Marohnic"], "start_timestamp": "13:11", "end_timestamp": "13:11", "podcast_index": 84, "episode_name": "Ep 84: Viktor Marohnic | User Acquisition and Lowering the Point of Entry | 57 Hours", "slug": "ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "57 Hours is a business that connects customers with local guides for adventures. Viktor Marohnic, the Founder and CEO, joins me on the podcast to discuss the challenges they have faced in streamlining their customer acquisition pipeline.", "podcast_length": "44:106"}, {"id": "431ee188-9075-46e3-a7ac-cd11255861d0", "episode_id": "14be81d4-1448-43e9-aa98-872c817c1655", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487a", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Cole Heilborn (17:27): Yeah, interesting. So I got to imagine a lot of your customers see an ad like that, but immediately they have a bunch of questions and you've inspired them with the photo or the video, but then there's the logistical portion of how do I do this? Which probably seems like a really big question to answer. So were you finding before you started hosting these webinars that a lot of potential customers would just fall off the map? There was no way for them to ease themselves into the funnel?\n\nViktor Marohnic (17:59): Yeah, exactly. I mean things that we are selling, it's like even let's say mountain biking in Moab to most of the people that are not really super experienced mountain bikers, it sounds scary. They're not sure if there are skill level, if they're ready to do that, how much fitness they need to have, that kind of thing. So what we try to do is on those webinars, we talk to guides, we have them on webinars, but we also bring clients and clients are typically like software developer from la, investment banker from Florida. And then we try to get those people to talk. I signed up for the trip and I loved it and I go to the gym two times a week and I was totally able to do this. That kind of lowers, removes that kind of fear factor and then people are more willing to actually commit and buy this. So I think we guess that it might be a problem, but this is definitely a proof that that's the way to go. Now when we are quite successful with these webinars series and a lot of people are converting after they participate in the webinar, but now we need to implement that strategy pretty much across the board everywhere because people are dropping off because they don't think they're ready. They have specific questions, they want to meet the guide, they want to maybe meet people that were there last year, that kind of things. Yeah,\n\nCole Heilborn (19:36): Interesting. And since implementing the webinars, how have you seen your conversion rate change?", "token_estimate": 501, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Viktor Marohnic"], "start_timestamp": "17:27", "end_timestamp": "19:36", "podcast_index": 84, "episode_name": "Ep 84: Viktor Marohnic | User Acquisition and Lowering the Point of Entry | 57 Hours", "slug": "ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "57 Hours is a business that connects customers with local guides for adventures. Viktor Marohnic, the Founder and CEO, joins me on the podcast to discuss the challenges they have faced in streamlining their customer acquisition pipeline.", "podcast_length": "44:106"}, {"id": "cd75ee4b-6f64-48b8-af35-96ed17defd0b", "episode_id": "14be81d4-1448-43e9-aa98-872c817c1655", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487a", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Viktor Marohnic (19:44): So first of all, if you are trying to sell the trip climbing in Thailand, which might be that's a package of a week long trip, it's probably going to cost, I don't know, I kind of made it up. We have a climbing trip in Sardinia, which I know that costs around $2,500. So if you try to advertise directly for that, your user acquisition costs can be between a hundred to a few hundred dollars per purchase. Actually per customer that actually bought, booked a trip for webinars, our lead acquisition is like two to $10 per lead because all they need to do is just sign up for a webinar, they just leave an email and then even if they don't participate in the webinar, you can retarget them later because we record the webinar, we compare that into a shorter video, we publish it on YouTube, you can target that user, say, Hey, you weren't there, but here you can take a look on YouTube and you get a thousand. We would get between 200 and a thousand participants of those webinars and then you convert, I dunno exact numbers, but you convert a certain number of, I think one number on some webinars we would convert around 5% of participants. So if you have a thousand people, 5%, there's like 50. Yeah, no, that's more a hundred. What's 5% of today?\n\nCole Heilborn (21:21): I think math was never my strong suit. Hundreds, I guess. Yeah. And so how does something like 5% compare to e-commerce?", "token_estimate": 355, "speakers": ["Viktor Marohnic", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "19:44", "end_timestamp": "21:21", "podcast_index": 84, "episode_name": "Ep 84: Viktor Marohnic | User Acquisition and Lowering the Point of Entry | 57 Hours", "slug": "ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "57 Hours is a business that connects customers with local guides for adventures. Viktor Marohnic, the Founder and CEO, joins me on the podcast to discuss the challenges they have faced in streamlining their customer acquisition pipeline.", "podcast_length": "44:106"}, {"id": "996d5d14-ff9a-448e-be76-4ba1d9563ef2", "episode_id": "14be81d4-1448-43e9-aa98-872c817c1655", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487a", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Viktor Marohnic (21:35): I think 5% I think, I don't know. It really depends on, the problem is that this is not a typical e-commerce. Again, it's a much, our average order value is around 1500 bucks, so it's expensive. So I would've to take a look, I know that there is something around one 2% conversion rate on e-commerce websites or something like that. It's like maybe 3% is really good, so 5% is fine, but you already filtered out a bunch of people. This is already people that signed up for a webinar. So I would have to look like how many people we targeted, how many people we actually saw them that calculate exact conversion point to be able to compare that. But we figure out, it's really hard for us to compare. I mean if you look at the data we need to look at, not really in e-commerce, we need to look at travel, I think vertical and see what conversions are over there because I think we are closer to that than typical eCommerce.\n\nCole Heilborn (22:41): Gotcha. And yeah, compared to that, what are you seeing?", "token_estimate": 257, "speakers": ["Viktor Marohnic", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "21:35", "end_timestamp": "22:41", "podcast_index": 84, "episode_name": "Ep 84: Viktor Marohnic | User Acquisition and Lowering the Point of Entry | 57 Hours", "slug": "ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "57 Hours is a business that connects customers with local guides for adventures. Viktor Marohnic, the Founder and CEO, joins me on the podcast to discuss the challenges they have faced in streamlining their customer acquisition pipeline.", "podcast_length": "44:106"}, {"id": "0146b374-cb0c-4fcd-a9c5-8a7fcc1aee8f", "episode_id": "14be81d4-1448-43e9-aa98-872c817c1655", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487a", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Viktor Marohnic (22:44): I don't know really. I don't know what the numbers are. What's the industry standard right now? I know that we are now breaking even and we are happy with that on that user acquisition costs and now it's on us to figure out how to increase the lifetime value of the customer. Because at the end, this whole thing is about having lifetime value of the customer higher than your user acquisition costs. And we believe some early data because we only have data for the last 12 months, but we believe that about third of our bookings is going to come from repeat customers because most of the folks that are booking through us, those are people that are passionate about outdoors. They're typically doing something in the outdoors or planning a trip at least once a year, quite often, like multiple times a year. And if you can make sure that we reengage, keep those customers and if we have enough inventory for them that we can offer, we believe that our lifetime value is going to be big, but it's early for us to say right now what's exactly lifetime value is. So breaking even or being close to breaking even on the first booking, it's great because it's quite likely that we count about third of that audience is going to book another trip in the next 12 months.\n\nCole Heilborn (24:17): Yeah, interesting. How do you guys go about calculating the lifetime value of a customer? Do you have an idea of how many of these types of trips or experiences your ideal customer would take?", "token_estimate": 377, "speakers": ["Viktor Marohnic", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "22:44", "end_timestamp": "24:17", "podcast_index": 84, "episode_name": "Ep 84: Viktor Marohnic | User Acquisition and Lowering the Point of Entry | 57 Hours", "slug": "ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "57 Hours is a business that connects customers with local guides for adventures. Viktor Marohnic, the Founder and CEO, joins me on the podcast to discuss the challenges they have faced in streamlining their customer acquisition pipeline.", "podcast_length": "44:106"}, {"id": "1cbe6f1f-a1d5-4df6-baab-9ff994115aad", "episode_id": "14be81d4-1448-43e9-aa98-872c817c1655", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487a", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Viktor Marohnic (24:31): Yeah, I mean on extreme end you have people that are booking 10 trips a year. So typically you would have, let's say a mountain biking trip, a kite surfing a week of kite surfing in Dominican Republic. And I dunno, in the winter, backcountry skiing, a couple of days of backcountry skiing. And then in the meantime you might go for a one day of skiing next to your resort or one day of climbing or you might take a lesson. So that's kind of extreme. And then on the lower end, people are planning at least one big trip a year. They will go if they're climbers or like mountain bikers, they'll go and mountain bike in whistle one year next year they're going to go, I dunno, to Croatia. So for us, even if they book one trip a year would be great. This is where we are now. If third of our audience would book one trip every year, we actually would be in a pretty good place. But again, we need to prove the data, we need to see where is it going to be. But I mean if you calculating lifetime value, it's kind of like, let's say that on average a third of your audience is booking two or three tips a year, one big trip to a couple of smaller trips, you're talking about between three and $6,000 spent on that kind of stuff on a yearly basis.\n\nCole Heilborn (26:16): And what'd you say the acquisition of that customer might be?\n\nViktor Marohnic (26:21): I think right now it's around, on average it's around 150. Depends month to month. Sometimes it's 80, sometimes it gets closer to 200, depends. We know which trip we are promoting, but on average I think we are averaging around a hundred and a hundred bucks per user.\n\nCole Heilborn (26:39): Gotcha. When you're finding folks to sign up for these webinars, I'm curious, how do you let people know about these webinars? How do people sign up for those? Have you learned anything?", "token_estimate": 465, "speakers": ["Viktor Marohnic", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "24:31", "end_timestamp": "26:39", "podcast_index": 84, "episode_name": "Ep 84: Viktor Marohnic | User Acquisition and Lowering the Point of Entry | 57 Hours", "slug": "ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "57 Hours is a business that connects customers with local guides for adventures. Viktor Marohnic, the Founder and CEO, joins me on the podcast to discuss the challenges they have faced in streamlining their customer acquisition pipeline.", "podcast_length": "44:106"}, {"id": "9d3b8407-5a58-46ce-bc0b-785b659b6874", "episode_id": "14be81d4-1448-43e9-aa98-872c817c1655", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487a", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Viktor Marohnic (26:48): It's just Facebook ad pretty much. And you're just targeting people that are, if it's a webinar on hiking in Iceland, you target people that like hikers or maybe they were looking at some kind of, maybe they're interested in going to Iceland. But I mean mainly you're targeting hikers because people that are into hiking and backpacking will look to do one trip a year pretty much.\n\nCole Heilborn (27:19): Gotcha.\n\nViktor Marohnic (27:20): And we did a couple of a few more things with webinars. We partnered up with some media publications. So for example, we had a webinar, I think the backpacking trip, we actually partnered with Conde Nest on one of their magazines and then some climbing trips we did with Optimist Magazine, free Skier Magazine, we promoted a couple of ski related webinars. So it's a combination of us doing social outreach plus them promoting that to their audience. That would pretty much, we would sold out a webinar in a couple of days.\n\nCole Heilborn (28:05): And I think you mentioned that you guys are taking these webinars and turning this into content for YouTube and other platforms?", "token_estimate": 286, "speakers": ["Viktor Marohnic", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "26:48", "end_timestamp": "28:05", "podcast_index": 84, "episode_name": "Ep 84: Viktor Marohnic | User Acquisition and Lowering the Point of Entry | 57 Hours", "slug": "ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "57 Hours is a business that connects customers with local guides for adventures. Viktor Marohnic, the Founder and CEO, joins me on the podcast to discuss the challenges they have faced in streamlining their customer acquisition pipeline.", "podcast_length": "44:106"}, {"id": "b3a8a2a1-9b66-4d88-9866-4aa55feae76e", "episode_id": "14be81d4-1448-43e9-aa98-872c817c1655", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487a", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Viktor Marohnic (28:14): Yeah, I mean the problem with the webinar, if you look at the, it's extremely high engagement rate. So just to give you an idea, typically in the system that on webinars is that half of your audience is going to drop off after 30 minutes. So 50% of the people will just drop off after first 30 minutes. For us, 95% of participants would stay for a full hour, which the webinar was supposed to last one hour and then 85% of that same group, and you're talking about a thousand people would stay for additional hour, which wasn't even scheduled. So they actually just stayed for another hour, ask a bunch of questions, talk to the guide, so super high engagement rate and then about 5% of those will actually go and buy the trip. The problem is that this is a onetime event and you have to repeat it over and over again. (29:10): So we started playing around with idea, I mean we are doing it. We started publishing that content, recording a little bit higher quality video and audio, and then we have a guy who is editing the video and publishing. So it's typically like hour, hour and a half long video originally. So we tried to cut it down to 40 minutes and then we publish it on YouTube. And what we've seen so far is YouTube is having above average engagement rate. I dunno how many percent of the folks would actually watch the whole video is three times better than industry standard, but it's early to say if that's going to be a big lead generation. I mean we've been doing it only for a couple of months and we've seen some leads coming through YouTube, but I think there is much more we can do over there. But anyway, trying to convert that one time content into a long-term channel, that's kind of the idea.\n\nCole Heilborn (30:17): Yeah, gotcha. The attention rate that you're getting in your webinars seems quite impressive. What do you attribute that to? Is there a format that you ask the guides to follow?", "token_estimate": 487, "speakers": ["Viktor Marohnic", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "28:14", "end_timestamp": "30:17", "podcast_index": 84, "episode_name": "Ep 84: Viktor Marohnic | User Acquisition and Lowering the Point of Entry | 57 Hours", "slug": "ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "57 Hours is a business that connects customers with local guides for adventures. Viktor Marohnic, the Founder and CEO, joins me on the podcast to discuss the challenges they have faced in streamlining their customer acquisition pipeline.", "podcast_length": "44:106"}, {"id": "cc6dc808-216e-4247-8416-acc2ff3b985d", "episode_id": "14be81d4-1448-43e9-aa98-872c817c1655", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487a", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Viktor Marohnic (30:28): I think we really select the people that are doing these webinars. It's a local guide and typically that guide is super passionate about that trip that somebody who lives in that area loves probably moved, I dunno, to that mountain or wherever from who knows where because they fell in love with certain area. So they're super passionate about that. And then also they're talking about some local stuff, local beta, local experience that you cannot really find anywhere else. I think it's authentic. We do quite a few prep meetings with guides and we make sure that they show the right things and slides and the story flows and all that stuff. So yeah, it's basically, and nobody else is doing it. It's like if you want to research mountain biking in, I dunno, New Zealand, I dunno, you're going to go to discussion boards, blogs, you don't know who these folks are. It's really hard to find that local beta. And we actually bring the guy from New Zealand who is mountain biker for the last 15 years and he talks about why, what's so special about mountain biking in New Zealand, for example.\n\nCole Heilborn (31:52): Sure. I think about the places, the times that I've traveled to go somewhere for an activity, I'll often just YouTube it and find someone who's been there. I know in the mountain bike world there's lots of riders who travel and post videos about their travels and you can kind of get some of the inside boots on the ground scoop from those types of individuals.", "token_estimate": 376, "speakers": ["Viktor Marohnic", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "30:28", "end_timestamp": "31:52", "podcast_index": 84, "episode_name": "Ep 84: Viktor Marohnic | User Acquisition and Lowering the Point of Entry | 57 Hours", "slug": "ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "57 Hours is a business that connects customers with local guides for adventures. Viktor Marohnic, the Founder and CEO, joins me on the podcast to discuss the challenges they have faced in streamlining their customer acquisition pipeline.", "podcast_length": "44:106"}, {"id": "b6163a03-f5b6-4b1d-a1a5-ad78db5aae99", "episode_id": "14be81d4-1448-43e9-aa98-872c817c1655", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487a", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Viktor Marohnic (32:15): Yeah, nobody's doing it If you're a mountain biker or I'm a climber for example, there is so many videos out there from climbers and you can see that this production is just getting bigger and bigger. More and more climbers are doing high-end videos, which is cool, but it doesn't speak to me at all. I cannot relate with whatever Adam Andre is climbing because just like I'll never go to that place. What I'm going to do in ANA or whatever in Spain, I can't climb anything over there. So I think people are hungry for videos that are coming from folks like me. I don't want to sit there and watch 40 minutes of father man talking about his, I want to see, I dunno, an entrepreneur from Croatia doing a trip in New Zealand and I want see that experience because that's kind of like, okay, I know I can do this. I think that is why the engagement, right, because you can't really find that kind of content anywhere else\n\nCole Heilborn (33:21): Just because it's relatable.\n\nViktor Marohnic (33:23): Yeah, I think so. Yeah. I mean even there are a couple of details that I was insisting on. Even if you're talking to a guy, let's say that guy in New Zealand mountain biker and he's a guy, even that is going to sound too extreme to you. The guy might say, yeah, went there and we, it's an easy trail whatever, I don't trust that guy. I mean maybe the trust is not the right word. I cannot relate to that because that's his profession. He's out there all the time. I don't know if I can do that. So we insisted on bringing clients. So we typically on webinar we'll try to get one client to talk together with a guide and that client needs to be somebody like a investment banker, software developer. And then when I hear that guy talking like, yeah, we went there, I was a little bit scared but it turned out it's not so hard, then I'm good. Then I feel confident that I can actually do it. Yeah,", "token_estimate": 480, "speakers": ["Viktor Marohnic", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "32:15", "end_timestamp": "33:23", "podcast_index": 84, "episode_name": "Ep 84: Viktor Marohnic | User Acquisition and Lowering the Point of Entry | 57 Hours", "slug": "ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "57 Hours is a business that connects customers with local guides for adventures. Viktor Marohnic, the Founder and CEO, joins me on the podcast to discuss the challenges they have faced in streamlining their customer acquisition pipeline.", "podcast_length": "44:106"}, {"id": "608b7b2d-c091-4cb2-9c69-d4582bdec301", "episode_id": "14be81d4-1448-43e9-aa98-872c817c1655", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487a", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Cole Heilborn (34:24): Interesting. So I'm trying to think of other industries that maybe have a similar challenge. The product is a high ticket item and the first one that comes to mind is the cruise ship industry. I don't know if that's exactly relatable, but I'm curious, do you know what an industry like that does? I imagine they face a similar challenge maybe.\n\nViktor Marohnic (34:48): Yeah, I don't know. I dunno about that specific industry, but when you said that I was thinking maybe about travel, all these travel blogs and even TV travel shows, typically it's a regular guy traveling around the world doing stuff and I think this is why people like it because it's just normal guy going to China and I dunno eating food at local markets and I was like, okay, this sounds great. I think you feel more comfortable booking that kind of trip or playing that kind of trip and you see something like that. So even if you look at travel blogs and lots of people just living out blogging about travel, I think that's probably what we need to do even more because these sports, they still sound, even if you're a good skier and you're skiing all of your life, a big country skiing trip in British Columbia might sound too extreme to you. And I did a couple of trips like that and I've seen people that are not great skiers and beginner skiers and they had amazing time. So we need to bring those kind of experiences to our audience and say, Hey, you can totally do this. I think you need to lower the barrier to entry because you can't expect people to book to commit to that kind of trip without having some interaction or having enough material that they can watch or read before they're making this decision. Yeah.\n\nCole Heilborn (36:30): So I'm curious then with the lessons that you've been learning from this, what other opportunities do you see outside of the travel, the experience industry for maybe folks who are in the e-commerce industry? What does this experiment tell you about ways other tactics or other sectors?", "token_estimate": 508, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Viktor Marohnic"], "start_timestamp": "34:24", "end_timestamp": "36:30", "podcast_index": 84, "episode_name": "Ep 84: Viktor Marohnic | User Acquisition and Lowering the Point of Entry | 57 Hours", "slug": "ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "57 Hours is a business that connects customers with local guides for adventures. Viktor Marohnic, the Founder and CEO, joins me on the podcast to discuss the challenges they have faced in streamlining their customer acquisition pipeline.", "podcast_length": "44:106"}, {"id": "0b1c756a-d315-45fa-8e52-5dd8eb6108ac", "episode_id": "14be81d4-1448-43e9-aa98-872c817c1655", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487a", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Viktor Marohnic (36:54): What's very different with 57 hours is I think it's typically the e-commerce. If you're building one product you're targeting, I have a friend, they're building a wearable for women. It's kind of like a Fitbit, but it's targeting women like a fitness necklace or I build a company that was targeting mobile app developers and basically you are bidding for one set of keywords. Well narrow how to build a mobile app, sell service, mobile app building, whatever. And that's it. You have to compete with a bunch of companies on that specific keyword and once when you win that position, you procure an unlimited potential there because the volume is so big for us it's very different once when we win position back country skiing in shaman, great, but that's going to generate a few hundred bookings a year, which is nice, but we have thousands and thousands of bookings, so we need to come up with new locations. So I think that's very different. And I dunno which industry has the same problem. Typically when you're talking about e-commerce, you're building content around single set of keywords and trying to dominate that kind of keyword and figuring out how to get that traffic and bit other. So I think I just had a call with somebody and they mentioned that you should look at Yelp or what was the other example, the companies that are doing automating bunch of SEO and trying to capture that long tail volume. That's interesting.\n\nCole Heilborn (38:40): This idea of lowering the barrier, adding a step in the process. How could other industries or other companies who maybe don't offer the same type of service or product that you do, how could they utilize this idea of enlarging or expanding the customer journey process?", "token_estimate": 439, "speakers": ["Viktor Marohnic", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "36:54", "end_timestamp": "38:40", "podcast_index": 84, "episode_name": "Ep 84: Viktor Marohnic | User Acquisition and Lowering the Point of Entry | 57 Hours", "slug": "ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "57 Hours is a business that connects customers with local guides for adventures. Viktor Marohnic, the Founder and CEO, joins me on the podcast to discuss the challenges they have faced in streamlining their customer acquisition pipeline.", "podcast_length": "44:106"}, {"id": "f8690e5b-bbc5-4c06-b6b3-153264be97fb", "episode_id": "14be81d4-1448-43e9-aa98-872c817c1655", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487a", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Viktor Marohnic (39:00): I think for me it was like you have to be patient. For me it was like I would love to, my initial drive was like, we need to figure out a way how we can get that kind of clear funnel working right away. You promote something, people come in, they buy and you have clear everything happens in a single session or whatever you can attribute, but it doesn't work here. You have to figure out, you have to work on two steps, one step, okay, just sign 'em off for whatever webinar, email. This strategy to capturing email is it is like, okay, we are doing webinars, some people are doing white papers, materials, all kind of stuff. But basically capturing that lead and you are lowering the barrier to entry and then you work through that audience. What we do, I never liked those strategies where we're just capturing, focusing on capturing email. (40:00): We try to provide some value to our customers right away for free. So that webinar is something that we produce and it's valuable and we don't charge for that. But I think it develops trust. People start, first of all, they learn about 57 hours, they understand how we operate, they know that we work with local guides and know that we put a lot of effort into creating good quality content and then they feel more comfortable booking through us next time, next time they're going to think about, okay, I might whatever book a ski trip, they're going to come to 57 hours and look for ski trip through us.\n\nCole Heilborn (40:38): Yeah, yeah. Well I think the lessons you guys have been learning are pretty unique and you talk about this idea of providing value and lowering the barrier to entry. I feel like those are things that we hear a lot about in the industry and hearing about how you guys have had to make it work for your version of what you guys offer as a company is pretty special. So we're coming up on the end of our time for today, but do you have any other lasting thoughts or anything you want to leave the audience with around anything we've discussed or I guess not that we've discussed either?", "token_estimate": 523, "speakers": ["Viktor Marohnic", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "39:00", "end_timestamp": "40:38", "podcast_index": 84, "episode_name": "Ep 84: Viktor Marohnic | User Acquisition and Lowering the Point of Entry | 57 Hours", "slug": "ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "57 Hours is a business that connects customers with local guides for adventures. Viktor Marohnic, the Founder and CEO, joins me on the podcast to discuss the challenges they have faced in streamlining their customer acquisition pipeline.", "podcast_length": "44:106"}, {"id": "61ec6293-ad1e-4343-a12c-aec95e8a791a", "episode_id": "14be81d4-1448-43e9-aa98-872c817c1655", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487a", "chunk_index": 19, "content": "Viktor Marohnic (41:14): Well, I'm actually reading this very interesting book and I think my lesson, okay, I actually have one kind of lesson that I learned hard way and I'm reading this book, which is kind of on the same line, along the same lines. It's called Traction and it's from the guy who founded Duck, duck, duck go. It's a blue cover on Amazon. There is another attraction book which is not the one listed, and he talks about something that he says that you have 19 different channels where you can acquire your customers email, marketing, social, whatever, like 19. And he typically said that seven stages of the company one will work. And then once when you figure that one on average you should double down and triple down the effort and just milk that channel as much as you can. And then at some point you're going to reach the point where this channel is not generating enough leads for you anymore and then you have to find something else. At my agency, we had something that we called when it comes to product development and product strategy and product design and everybody's talking about iterations and how do you have to iterate and all constantly improve. (42:30): We learn that you never know what's going to work what not. You can have the smartest people in the room and they have my great idea and you launch it and it's a complete fail and then you launch something else that you are kind of all, I dunno, let's do it. But we are not sure and it works great. This webinar thing, for example, for us, we did it because basically it was pandemic guides were at home. It was like, okay, let's try this. So we kind of almost accidentally discovered it. So you never know. And so at my agency we had something, we coined the term which we called Experimentational velocity. (43:07): And basically every time you try something new, you should consider that being an experiment or some people call that thing a bet. You're betting on something so you have to play small bets. That bet needs to be an effort, which is like two weeks a month and what's happen if your experimentation or velocity is high. So you have a company that is doing two experiments a month, so they did 24, they tried 24 different things in a year. Or you have a company that tried two things, one that year who is more likely to succeed. And I think it's all about that, just trying things super fast, finding a way how you can test out the ideas cheap and quickly. And I think that's a recipe to success because you can hire the most expensive, most smart people, they don't know anything. Whoever tells you that they know how to do this, they're lying or I dunno, whatever. (44:08): But I think we approach everything that we don't know. This was my agency as well. We would go to our clients and we would say, Hey, we don't know. We don't know your market, we don't know your problem, but we have methods, we have process that help us lower the risks, test out the ideas and find the winning idea winning strategy. And I think that's, if you approach it that way, if you are too arrogant and you think you are right and you never want to give up on your idea, you're probably going to fail. I dunno if that's, that's typically what I tell to most of the folks that are starting these days because I spent 10 years building a product and I was stubborn to change anything. And it's painful. It was painful at the end.", "token_estimate": 854, "speakers": ["Viktor Marohnic"], "start_timestamp": "41:14", "end_timestamp": "41:14", "podcast_index": 84, "episode_name": "Ep 84: Viktor Marohnic | User Acquisition and Lowering the Point of Entry | 57 Hours", "slug": "ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "57 Hours is a business that connects customers with local guides for adventures. Viktor Marohnic, the Founder and CEO, joins me on the podcast to discuss the challenges they have faced in streamlining their customer acquisition pipeline.", "podcast_length": "44:106"}, {"id": "22a47cef-d256-4d7d-b933-99a37c2e6a33", "episode_id": "14be81d4-1448-43e9-aa98-872c817c1655", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487a", "chunk_index": 20, "content": "Cole Heilborn (44:56): No, well said. So I'm curious now, what other bets are you placing? What other experiments are you guys working on?\n\nViktor Marohnic (45:03): I mean it's like all kinds of stuff. Referral programs, how to motivate guides to spread the word about 87 hours referrals, what kind of loyalty program, what kind of email marketing, all kind of small things. Wherever we can improve the lifetime value kind of. And then from product, how to make every page, how to increase the conversion. So somebody say, I dunno, the pricing is not clear and we change it and we see if it improve the conversions or not. Or check out process is too complicated. Okay, let's try to simplify it or then, I dunno, FAQ doesn't cover most, it's too long or it covers different bronch questions. So we are constantly photos and maybe not where they need to be. I mean all kind of stuff. We are doing ton of ab testings and trying out a bunch of ideas. And then when it comes to advertising from copy, from photos, from videos, that's pretty much happens on every day.\n\nCole Heilborn (46:14): Yeah. Well what a great testament to the idea of trying new things and just keep trying things and seeing what works. Again, I appreciate you taking the time to share the journey and the lessons that you guys have been learning. If folks want to follow along with you personally with 57 Hours or your agency, where can people go to learn more?\n\nViktor Marohnic (46:35): My email is Victor with k victor@fiftysevenhours.com. You can reach out to me, I'm on Instagram as well, Facebook, LinkedIn. Feel free to reach out and I'll be happy to have a chat and help if I can. Yeah.\n\nCole Heilborn (46:50): Awesome. And last question, what is one of the trips or experiences that you've taken through 57 hours that you'd recommend? What's the best one?", "token_estimate": 459, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Viktor Marohnic"], "start_timestamp": "44:56", "end_timestamp": "46:50", "podcast_index": 84, "episode_name": "Ep 84: Viktor Marohnic | User Acquisition and Lowering the Point of Entry | 57 Hours", "slug": "ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "57 Hours is a business that connects customers with local guides for adventures. Viktor Marohnic, the Founder and CEO, joins me on the podcast to discuss the challenges they have faced in streamlining their customer acquisition pipeline.", "podcast_length": "44:106"}, {"id": "6e18d80e-adb9-4710-b944-7579ec95eb9a", "episode_id": "14be81d4-1448-43e9-aa98-872c817c1655", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487a", "chunk_index": 21, "content": "Viktor Marohnic (47:01): I'm a big skier and probably my favorite trip that we have on 57 hours is a hot based bay country skiing in British Columbia. They fly with a helicopter to this remote hu with 10 other people, chef who cooks for you and then you basically spend ski touring and you don't see anybody else besides your group because it's just a huge place and there are so much mountains over there and it's not expensive. It's not going to cost you more than your regular ski resort trip. And British Columbia has the best snow in the world in my opinion.\n\nCole Heilborn (47:45): Awesome. Well sounds good, Victor. Thanks again. And yeah, have a good, great rest of your day. No worries.\n\nViktor Marohnic (47:50): Thanks so much.\n\nCole Heilborn (47:51): Alright, see you. Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learned something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 248, "speakers": ["Viktor Marohnic", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "47:01", "end_timestamp": "47:51", "podcast_index": 84, "episode_name": "Ep 84: Viktor Marohnic | User Acquisition and Lowering the Point of Entry | 57 Hours", "slug": "ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-84-viktor-marohnic-user-acquisition-and-lowering-the-point-of-entry-57-hours", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "57 Hours is a business that connects customers with local guides for adventures. Viktor Marohnic, the Founder and CEO, joins me on the podcast to discuss the challenges they have faced in streamlining their customer acquisition pipeline.", "podcast_length": "44:106"}, {"id": "b33cb769-531d-4136-a8ed-05c2bad3044b", "episode_id": "a7fea902-91ab-4add-9e95-c6224f4d3d0c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487b", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Nicole Handel (00:00:03): In a world where humans are one of the driving forces of all things like it or not, then psychology is everything and is everywhere. And we certainly do things that are subconscious, but that's psychological, like everything we do from the product purchases we make, but also why we do anything that is all psychology.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:00:24): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Hilborn and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Portside Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Hilborn, and today I'm sitting down with Nicole Handel. She's the director of marketing over at Mammo Nicole, how are you doing?\n\nNicole Handel (00:01:06): Good, Cole, how are you doing?\n\nCole Heilborn (00:01:08): Fantastic. Thanks for joining us today. Where are you calling in from?\n\nNicole Handel (00:01:12): Yeah, thanks for having me. I'm in North Conway, New Hampshire.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:01:15): Okay, right on. You guys are based out of there or up there, correct?\n\nNicole Handel (00:01:20): Yeah, sort of the same general area. We have offices in Vermont and in Colorado, so not too far from where I live.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:01:29): Okay, right on. Well, I'm excited to have you on the show. I'm excited to dig into our topic today and just have a fun conversation. Before we jump in though, I'd love if you could kind of give us a quick introduction on yourself, how long you've been working with Mammo, what your job entails, that type of stuff.", "token_estimate": 490, "speakers": ["Nicole Handel", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:00:03", "end_timestamp": "00:01:29", "podcast_index": 85, "episode_name": "Ep 85: Nicole Handel | Consumer Psychology and Decision Making | MAMMUT", "slug": "ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Nicole Handel, the Director of Marketing at MAMMUT joins me to discuss psychology and the consumer's decision-making process.", "podcast_length": "44:107"}, {"id": "357881af-506c-42a7-946f-6b422818da9c", "episode_id": "a7fea902-91ab-4add-9e95-c6224f4d3d0c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487b", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Nicole Handel (00:01:48): Yeah, I'm excited to be here too. I'm a long time fan of the podcast, so it's cool to chat with you. I would say I'm most often identified as the owner of Two Dogs because my dogs are a pretty central piece of my life, so I do lots of different kind of outdoor sports with my dogs, trail running, mountain biking, climbing, split boarding, et cetera. And I have been with Mammo for I think just over a year and a half. So I haven't worked in this role that whole time. I've been in this role about seven months. But yeah, been with the brand for coming on two years now and really, really happy here. Really stoked on everything we're doing.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:02:34): And before your role as Director of marketing, what were you doing?\n\nNicole Handel (00:02:37): Yeah, I was managing direct to consumer key accounts, so lots of pro channels and that sort of thing.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:02:46): Okay, gotcha. And what are your dogs' names?\n\nNicole Handel (00:02:49): Bear and Ducks. One's a German Shepherd, one is a Dutch Shepherd mix, and they're really fun. They're actually both at my feet right now, so if you hear something that sounds like a dog, it's definitely a dog.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:03:02): Does your German Shepherd bark all of the time like mine does.\n\nNicole Handel (00:03:06): My German Shepherd is a sweet angel who does almost nothing wrong, but my Dutch Shepherd mix does bark all of the time at anything from creatures to inanimate objects, so I can relate to you there. That's pretty normal for the breed. They're definitely very barky and bear. My German Shepherd was my first dog and I just got so lucky because he's such a sweetheart and I thought, okay, I can do anything. And I got ducks. No, I can't do anything. He is like, same thing. You'll definitely hear him at some point, so totally relatable.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:03:43): Gotcha. Well, Nicole, I'm curious how you got interested in the field of marketing. I know you've studied psychology and that's what we're going to start to get into with this conversation today, but when were you introduced to the industry?", "token_estimate": 531, "speakers": ["Nicole Handel", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:01:48", "end_timestamp": "00:03:43", "podcast_index": 85, "episode_name": "Ep 85: Nicole Handel | Consumer Psychology and Decision Making | MAMMUT", "slug": "ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Nicole Handel, the Director of Marketing at MAMMUT joins me to discuss psychology and the consumer's decision-making process.", "podcast_length": "44:107"}, {"id": "a1e625dd-859c-4faf-85fb-b56a10e64e64", "episode_id": "a7fea902-91ab-4add-9e95-c6224f4d3d0c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487b", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Nicole Handel (00:03:58): Yeah, so marketing, I mean I think it's kind of funny, especially after the TV show Mad Men was released, marketing really has this reputation for being this kind of thing that anybody can do as long as you're a little bit creative or charismatic or something like that. So I think truly I feel like most people are interested in marketing and I was one of them. I studied psychology simply because I wanted to study all of these different things like anthropology, sociology, poetry, philosophy, and in my head at the time as a 17-year-old when you're deciding what to study, psychology seemed like the thing that was most likely to sound good on paper and get me a job. The irony of course, is that I actually don't work in the field of psychology, but I'm pretty convinced that having a degree in psychology is really beneficial to marketing. (00:04:55): So I was always interested in marketing and after I graduated college I really wanted to go on to study consumer anthropology or corporate anthropology, which is essentially why are consumers doing what they're doing, what is motivating them, how are they using products, how do they associate with a brand? And that kind of aspect of anthropology or any kind of study of people was really interesting to me. I ended up not going to grad school. I kind of inherited bear and my life changed a little bit and I bartended for a while. I taught spin classes and Pilates, and then I eventually ended up in a sales position working for the company that owns Jameson Irish whiskey, and there was a lot of marketing components to that role, and that's when I kind of started to develop some marketing specific skillset. And Instagram certainly I think has helped shape lot of people who have no formal background in marketing to be marketing savvy individuals. So I think that played a role as well.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:06:05): Yeah, well said. So as you're considering psychology in the classroom setting, what are some of the biggest things that you learned in school and that now apply to where you find yourself today?", "token_estimate": 525, "speakers": ["Nicole Handel", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:03:58", "end_timestamp": "00:06:05", "podcast_index": 85, "episode_name": "Ep 85: Nicole Handel | Consumer Psychology and Decision Making | MAMMUT", "slug": "ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Nicole Handel, the Director of Marketing at MAMMUT joins me to discuss psychology and the consumer's decision-making process.", "podcast_length": "44:107"}, {"id": "aeb0b7cc-6e7e-4d11-b313-ddf5e31fb531", "episode_id": "a7fea902-91ab-4add-9e95-c6224f4d3d0c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487b", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Nicole Handel (00:06:19): Oh, interesting. Well, my favorite learning that has stuck with me over the years as somebody who has a lot of opinions and is a fairly loud person, I find myself in conversations pretty often where people are sharing feelings and so it can be emotional. And one thing I learned actually in a mediation class was about position versus interest. So in any conversation, and this is relevant in how we speak to others when we consider things like sustainability and DEI, when we think about the political climate that we've been in this country for a while now, and to anybody who's currently studying psychology and who's fresher on this than me, I apologize in advance if I'm doing this concept of disservice. But position and interest is about what people are saying in a conversation or an argument or a debate, and then interest is what they mean. (00:07:21): What's at the root of that. So an example is, I don't know if we're talking about the environment and you say climate change isn't real, that's your position, but maybe your interest is you are saying that you don't want to believe that we've done this much damage to the earth because that doesn't serve you or your lifestyle or whatever the case is. That's really at the core of what you're saying. Or if somebody says, I don't like that you did this thing, what they mean is that hurt my feelings and it didn't feel good. So considering position versus interest comes up for me all the time in my personal and professional life because what people say isn't always what they mean. And anytime anybody says anything, there's a feeling behind that they mean something. And so finding what's meant over what's said is I think really powerful. And I know that wasn't so specific to marketing, but that's the thing. I mean, it's specific to just existence as a human who communicates. It's like the primary thing that has stuck with me. It's less sciencey and flashy, but it is interpersonal and I think that's important.", "token_estimate": 502, "speakers": ["Nicole Handel"], "start_timestamp": "00:06:19", "end_timestamp": "00:06:19", "podcast_index": 85, "episode_name": "Ep 85: Nicole Handel | Consumer Psychology and Decision Making | MAMMUT", "slug": "ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Nicole Handel, the Director of Marketing at MAMMUT joins me to discuss psychology and the consumer's decision-making process.", "podcast_length": "44:107"}, {"id": "054ee8c9-0fe9-4e3c-b693-8861d9a268c9", "episode_id": "a7fea902-91ab-4add-9e95-c6224f4d3d0c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487b", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:08:39): Yeah, so I mean personally that, well, personally and professionally, I suppose you could see that playing out frequently. Is that something you're cognizant about while you're doing your job, while you're making decisions, while you're putting together messaging?\n\nNicole Handel (00:08:54): Yeah, totally. I mentioned earlier, I don't know if somebody doesn't agree with something we've said or done on social media, it's easy to say why do they have to be confrontational when we are trying to do a good thing that's a human feeling or emotion that's easy to feel in a case like that. But then you consider what's the interest there? They're concerned about safety, they want the best for others. They want to make sure that information being shared is accurate. That comes up. That certainly comes up. And anybody who works with people has to keep that in mind. It's super relevant for I think just anybody who has to function in a group.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:09:39): So it sounds like the position statement is where people can disagree, but the interest perspective is maybe the common ground that a lot of people find themselves feeling the same way,\n\nNicole Handel (00:09:51): Same way? Well, it's irrefutable. You can disagree with a position you can't disagree with, I feel X, I experienced X that can't be argued with, which is really why that's a much stronger way to communicate in the first place if you can manage. But yeah, you're totally right. Interest is the common ground and interest is the thing that anybody can understand. Maybe I can't understand why you voted for a certain candidate, but I can understand that you had feelings about something that that candidate was referring to. I can understand that you have a vested interest in your loved ones and maybe that can, that's the thing that we can all relate to.", "token_estimate": 466, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Nicole Handel"], "start_timestamp": "00:08:39", "end_timestamp": "00:09:51", "podcast_index": 85, "episode_name": "Ep 85: Nicole Handel | Consumer Psychology and Decision Making | MAMMUT", "slug": "ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Nicole Handel, the Director of Marketing at MAMMUT joins me to discuss psychology and the consumer's decision-making process.", "podcast_length": "44:107"}, {"id": "9424317a-4cad-4276-a2e1-4356db5015dc", "episode_id": "a7fea902-91ab-4add-9e95-c6224f4d3d0c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487b", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:10:34): Well, I think about user group tensions like hikers versus mountain bikers or I don't know, mountain bikers versus equestrians, and their position is, well, horses get in the way or bikes get in the way, but their interest is, well, we all love to be outside and we all love to be either riding through the woods on a horse or riding through the woods on a bike,\n\nNicole Handel (00:10:58): And we all want to feel safe. That's the interest too, me saying, you just came out of nowhere with your horse while I was on this downhill section of my bike. What I'm really saying is I was scared that you and me were both unsafe in that situation. No one can argue with that. That's pretty understandable.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:11:17): So having an education in this stuff, do you feel like it makes your job harder or easier because I feel like you kind of have a shorthand or there's shortcuts that you understand that could make your life easier?\n\nNicole Handel (00:11:32): No, I think it makes life harder. I think I'm more prone to overthink everything I do or say because, and really I guess this is a great tie in because of position versus interest. When you understand these concepts, I think you're at risk of always worrying that someone's going to take your interest as position or not see the interest in your position and misunderstand or not. I think most people who study psychology, at least in my experiences at school, most people are overthinkers and highly sensitive and super emotional. And so I think that's an advantage in an interpersonal sense because I hope that it allows me to be a better communicator and understand those around me better. But it's a disadvantage in that you can't really turn it off. It's a programming thing.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:12:31): So when you were studying psychology in school, marketing wasn't on the radar. You just thought it was an interesting subject matter?", "token_estimate": 484, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Nicole Handel"], "start_timestamp": "00:10:34", "end_timestamp": "00:12:31", "podcast_index": 85, "episode_name": "Ep 85: Nicole Handel | Consumer Psychology and Decision Making | MAMMUT", "slug": "ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Nicole Handel, the Director of Marketing at MAMMUT joins me to discuss psychology and the consumer's decision-making process.", "podcast_length": "44:107"}, {"id": "475c6f60-f32a-472c-b64b-aa1f6d93f4b9", "episode_id": "a7fea902-91ab-4add-9e95-c6224f4d3d0c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487b", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Nicole Handel (00:12:37): Yeah, yeah, totally. And I think really less specifically marketing and more specifically consumer behavior was interesting to me. And really that is a huge portion of marketing. So never specifically marketing, but always sort of peripherally.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:12:53): What about consumer behavior was of interest?\n\nNicole Handel (00:12:56): So I mean, I'm interested in humans in general, and I'm always wondering why we do the things we do and what's motivating those decisions. And so for example, I'm sure we all know somebody who is an extreme brand loyalist to a particular brand. And if you ask them, and I experienced this at Jameson too, some people would say, for example, I only drink X whiskey. And I'd say, oh really? Well, what do you mean it's the best? Well, why is it the best? What makes it the best? And most people don't know. We don't have a firm grasp of why we like the things we like unless we actually consciously think about it because a lot of these things are just associations, emotional affiliations, whatever the case is. So that's particularly interesting to me, and I think that's where that interest kind of rolled into a more formal passion.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:13:58): So what about though trying to understand what's happening behind the scenes was of interest? Because I think in that instance, that example, there's something happening emotionally or neurologically in that individual and they don't realize why they're making the decisions they're making necessarily. But what about digging deeper is of interest?", "token_estimate": 396, "speakers": ["Nicole Handel", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:12:37", "end_timestamp": "00:13:58", "podcast_index": 85, "episode_name": "Ep 85: Nicole Handel | Consumer Psychology and Decision Making | MAMMUT", "slug": "ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Nicole Handel, the Director of Marketing at MAMMUT joins me to discuss psychology and the consumer's decision-making process.", "podcast_length": "44:107"}, {"id": "f2af5b53-15ec-40c1-bdf1-9ad0fdd786df", "episode_id": "a7fea902-91ab-4add-9e95-c6224f4d3d0c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487b", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Nicole Handel (00:14:20): Yeah, so I talk about this pretty often and not to stick with liquor industry examples, but I think this is a really good one, and we see it in the outdoor industry a lot as well. Tito's vodka for example, it it's vodka, the average consumer, you could put 20 different unflavored vodkas in front of them and they would not know the difference. But Tito's has taken this really strong stance as being a dog affiliated brand and they donate to dog rescues. And Subaru is another good example. They also donate a lot to dog rescues, and they have so clearly branded themselves as an outdoor brand, an outdoor vehicle. And in the case of the Tito's example that exactly what you said, that's an emotional affiliation. People are like, well, I love dogs, I love my dog, I love other dogs. And that kind of work that's being put in on the brand, because like I said, I don't think it thinks Tito tastes better than any other vodka, but it tastes better to them because it has this strong emotional association.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:15:28): Well, I think you just set us up really nicely for our conversation today. To recap, we're going to be kind of digging into psychology, human behavior, what informs our decisions and experiences both as consumers and as marketers, and ultimately this question of why do humans do what we do? So I guess let's buckle up and let's dive in because I feel like this is a pretty large topic but can also be dealt in the very granular. So in the day-to-day as people are going about their lives, what are some of the common psychological tactics or practices that marketers are deploying and people are just running into that you're maybe not aware of?", "token_estimate": 427, "speakers": ["Nicole Handel", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:14:20", "end_timestamp": "00:15:28", "podcast_index": 85, "episode_name": "Ep 85: Nicole Handel | Consumer Psychology and Decision Making | MAMMUT", "slug": "ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Nicole Handel, the Director of Marketing at MAMMUT joins me to discuss psychology and the consumer's decision-making process.", "podcast_length": "44:107"}, {"id": "057c5bce-a76b-4321-bff9-2800e297803b", "episode_id": "a7fea902-91ab-4add-9e95-c6224f4d3d0c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487b", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Nicole Handel (00:16:07): Yeah. Well, I think we already touched on the primary big picture one, which is emotion. I think that everything we do is informed by some emotion, even charitable act when we do something good for the environment or for someone else. I think, and this is not to be pessimistic, but just to be kind of scientific or realistic about it, are we doing that because we really care in a very selfless way about that thing? Or are we doing that because we want to believe that we are the type of person who would do something good? And really the conclusion I've come to is the answer doesn't matter if we're doing good, let it be doing good is still doing good. And I think we don't have to look at decision-making in that way apart from just having an interest in it. But I think back, kind of one step is emotion is the number one thing that I think all brands capitalize on or should or could capitalize on, and that's really big picture. (00:17:13): So we talked about dogs, people have a strong emotional affiliation to dogs campaigns that tap into negative emotions, for example, like diet culture, you don't want to be X or you don't want to feel X, or if you want to be better or whatever, or do this, buy this. And that's certainly emotional if anybody has any level of insecurity or self-loathing, which I think most of us have, some of that's an attractive campaign emotionally speaking. So I think mean without sounding like I'm simplifying, I think behind you were asking what are some kind of behind the scenes things and tactics? Emotion is probably everything. I mean, certainly there are other tactics like save money do this. But isn't that sort of an emotion too? You want to feel in control or you want to feel responsible financially. Of course, technically saving money is not emotional. That's the survival mechanism. But I think emotion is really at the root and at the core of all of the kind of tactics as you put it.", "token_estimate": 491, "speakers": ["Nicole Handel"], "start_timestamp": "00:16:07", "end_timestamp": "00:16:07", "podcast_index": 85, "episode_name": "Ep 85: Nicole Handel | Consumer Psychology and Decision Making | MAMMUT", "slug": "ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Nicole Handel, the Director of Marketing at MAMMUT joins me to discuss psychology and the consumer's decision-making process.", "podcast_length": "44:107"}, {"id": "e48b3af4-32aa-41cb-ad86-a5ba10e386dc", "episode_id": "a7fea902-91ab-4add-9e95-c6224f4d3d0c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487b", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:18:32): Man, that's awesome. That's awesome to hear. So this idea of psychology, I feel like it's kind of the backbone to a lot of the work that I think marketers do, and as we're considering the different outreach programs and the different programs that you guys are working on, psychology plays a role in all of this type of stuff. As you're going through your day to day, I think what I'm trying to understand is how much of a role are you actively thinking about the psychology of what you're doing versus how much of it is kind of passive and it's just unfolding naturally?\n\nNicole Handel (00:19:04): Yeah. Well, one thing I think that is important to mention is I think when we talk about psychology in marketing, the first thing people think is psychology as a dirty word. Like, oh, they're trying to scheme us or they're trying to pull one over on us. But really psychology and marketing, at least for us, because psychology isn't just how do I get somebody to do what I want them to do? That's certainly one factor in psychology, but psychology is also what does somebody want? What do they need? What will make them feel safe? What will let them know that they're welcome or what will help inform safe decisions for that? Those things are all psychology. So we certainly aren't huddling around in our marketing team meetings, like going through psychological research studies, but I would say it's more the latter, which is it just is in everything we do. I don't think you can work in marketing without tapping into psychology a little bit. And again, not necessarily in any kind of manipulative way, but more so figuring out if our goal is to help our consumers to bring more consumers in to create this community around the outdoors, then psychology has to be involved because we have to know how can we get people in and what can we do to be a leader in safety education in DEI and sustainability. So I think it's just sort of in the corner of everyone's minds.", "token_estimate": 499, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Nicole Handel"], "start_timestamp": "00:18:32", "end_timestamp": "00:19:04", "podcast_index": 85, "episode_name": "Ep 85: Nicole Handel | Consumer Psychology and Decision Making | MAMMUT", "slug": "ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Nicole Handel, the Director of Marketing at MAMMUT joins me to discuss psychology and the consumer's decision-making process.", "podcast_length": "44:107"}, {"id": "97737687-6d50-4b34-b873-e5bd64d48821", "episode_id": "a7fea902-91ab-4add-9e95-c6224f4d3d0c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487b", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:20:37): Yeah, interesting. So I'm curious in your guys' experience, so something I've been thinking about in preparation for our call today is the idea of colors and how colors influence emotion and create feelings. Obviously you could look at color on a billboard example or you could look at color on a piece of clothing and the color of that clothing choice. Are you guys, when you're designing clothing, are you thinking about the color choice and what that's going to do for the end consumer? Or is that not a question that's in the equation?\n\nNicole Handel (00:21:13): Yeah, that's an interesting question and not take everything that I'm saying on this with a grain of salt because I'm not on the design team, but I think in the outdoor industry, probably color and emotion are, well, let me backtrack. Not just in the outdoor industry, but for anything like technical or outdoor meaning not just lifestyle clothing, I imagine that the motivators for color choice are less emotional and probably more environmentally specific. For example, you've probably seen a photo of somebody, I don't know, skiing and if they're wearing black or white, probably the shot isn't all that. It could be, you probably more often see people skiing in red or yellow or whatever, any bright standout color. I think that's probably more a motivation for most outdoor activity brands as well as thinking. One thing, maybe this is slightly off topic, but one thing that comes up for us a lot at my moot is this concept of women don't just want pink outerwear. That definitely has been a standard pink or purple outerwear, a standard in our industry for a long time, and that's emotional. I mean, I think that's relevant here too, but I would say it's less about colors and the emotions that they emit and more like what environment is this used in and what makes sense?\n\nCole Heilborn (00:22:37): Gotcha. Sure. And there's also the very practical element, I suppose, of having a nice bright jacket", "token_estimate": 500, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Nicole Handel"], "start_timestamp": "00:20:37", "end_timestamp": "00:22:37", "podcast_index": 85, "episode_name": "Ep 85: Nicole Handel | Consumer Psychology and Decision Making | MAMMUT", "slug": "ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Nicole Handel, the Director of Marketing at MAMMUT joins me to discuss psychology and the consumer's decision-making process.", "podcast_length": "44:107"}, {"id": "b087462d-a963-4c76-9861-d0e333128c12", "episode_id": "a7fea902-91ab-4add-9e95-c6224f4d3d0c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487b", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Nicole Handel (00:22:44): Safety. Yeah, totally. It sounds silly, but psychology is everything and how do you talk about it in any kind of directed way when it's that big picture? But no, all good so far.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:22:57): So expand on that, what you just mentioned, this idea that psychology is everything in marketing.\n\nNicole Handel (00:23:03): Yeah, well, I even mean psychology is everything, because in a world where humans are one of the driving forces of all things, like it or not, if humans are the driving force of all things, then psychology is everything and is everywhere. And nothing that we do, we certainly do things that are subconscious, but that's psychological, like everything we do from the product purchases we make, but also why we do anything that is all psychology. So I think it is hard to talk about it in any kind specific way.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:23:47): When you guys were thinking about human behavior, I guess in your branding consumer department, how do you think about human behavior? What is that to you guys?\n\nNicole Handel (00:23:56): In terms of how we structure campaigns or how do you mean exactly?\n\nCole Heilborn (00:24:03): I guess across the board, maybe it looks differently for different applications.", "token_estimate": 314, "speakers": ["Nicole Handel", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:22:44", "end_timestamp": "00:24:03", "podcast_index": 85, "episode_name": "Ep 85: Nicole Handel | Consumer Psychology and Decision Making | MAMMUT", "slug": "ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Nicole Handel, the Director of Marketing at MAMMUT joins me to discuss psychology and the consumer's decision-making process.", "podcast_length": "44:107"}, {"id": "5e8c6384-9408-4abb-a0e3-30660c66a113", "episode_id": "a7fea902-91ab-4add-9e95-c6224f4d3d0c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487b", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Nicole Handel (00:24:08): Yeah, I think it definitely looks differently. For example, the sales department is thinking about human behavior in terms of sales conversions. I would say brand and consumer looks at human behavior in similar ways that we talked about earlier, which is what do our consumers want? What do they need to feel welcome? What are our consumers saying about our brand? What are they saying about other brands that they really like? That kind of thing. I think it sounds cheesy, but human behavior to the brand and consumer department should be through the lens of service. What is needed in the market, what is there not currently? We talk about this a lot, just to bring it down to a very digestible level. We talk about this a lot in regards to social media. (00:25:02): Before my whole team was together, we were looking at other brand social media and our past social media and different aspects of social media and what we were seeing there and what we see a lot of brands do. And as users of social media, I'm sure we've both seen this. We notice a lot of sort of mountain photo caption that's like, are you getting out into the mountains this weekend? And that's fine, there's nothing wrong with that. But if we're talking about brand and consumer being about human services, nobody really needs to see that. That doesn't really serve anybody. I'm guilty of doing that on my own social media, and we certainly still do that on that mood, social media, if we have a really beautiful photo that we want to share. But ultimately when we think about human behavior, it's through the lens of what's needed. And in the specific example of social media, what's needed are more resources, amplification of voices or organizations that need to be amplified, like who's doing good or who needs the word put out there? What is needed on that space and what isn't there enough of. So that's, I would say, the way that trickles down through the department.", "token_estimate": 493, "speakers": ["Nicole Handel"], "start_timestamp": "00:24:08", "end_timestamp": "00:24:08", "podcast_index": 85, "episode_name": "Ep 85: Nicole Handel | Consumer Psychology and Decision Making | MAMMUT", "slug": "ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Nicole Handel, the Director of Marketing at MAMMUT joins me to discuss psychology and the consumer's decision-making process.", "podcast_length": "44:107"}, {"id": "bf101ee0-b924-4d75-9a27-4fbbae35ffc5", "episode_id": "a7fea902-91ab-4add-9e95-c6224f4d3d0c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487b", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:26:20): Well, yeah, if I'm picking up what you're trying to communicate, it's that humans or the things on earth that make progress, that move things forwards, that move the needle. And if what drives us is psychology and how we think in our hierarchy of needs, then that's the mechanism that allows us to do the things that we do.\n\nNicole Handel (00:26:45): Totally. Yeah, spot on.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:26:48): Interesting. So that was another one of my questions, this idea of needs of Maslow's hierarchy of needs. I'm sure people have heard of it, but what are those different categories?\n\nNicole Handel (00:27:06): Oh, you're testing me now. I don't know off the top of my head. Could look it up, but safety, security, validation, basic needs like shelter, food, water, or\n\nCole Heilborn (00:27:21): Yeah, I think you summed it up. I was looking at 'em prior because I don't remember them off the top of my head. So the first one is psychological needs like food, shelter, clothing. The second is safety, third is love and belongingness, fourth is esteem, and fifth is self-actualization. Thinking about you guys, and I guess a lot of other brands in the outdoor industry, this idea of safety seems like a really primitive need that maybe you don't experience a lot if you're not outside in the elements, but you're talking about not wanting to pure slimy, but this idea of safety and the products and the solutions that you guys offer seems like a good fit there.", "token_estimate": 371, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Nicole Handel"], "start_timestamp": "00:26:20", "end_timestamp": "00:27:21", "podcast_index": 85, "episode_name": "Ep 85: Nicole Handel | Consumer Psychology and Decision Making | MAMMUT", "slug": "ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Nicole Handel, the Director of Marketing at MAMMUT joins me to discuss psychology and the consumer's decision-making process.", "podcast_length": "44:107"}, {"id": "73b1c736-2957-44aa-9c8f-345aee598d23", "episode_id": "a7fea902-91ab-4add-9e95-c6224f4d3d0c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487b", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Nicole Handel (00:28:05): Yeah, so it's interesting because everybody, your point about not everybody being consciously aware of needing safety unless they're in these high risk situations, in Maslow's hierarchy of needs, safety refers to obviously so much more than that. We all need to feel safe, whatever that means. And to your point, I think totally it's ironic that on the hierarchy of needs safety is orange. Well, it's not ironic, it's intentional. In our industry and in other industries, safety is the color of orange, and we have a color safety orange that denotes your avalanche safety tool pocket in our packs, et cetera. But I think you're right. Safety is something that everybody wants to feel and needs to feel because safety at the end of the day means survival. Safety is life. And being a brand that makes lifesaving products specifically airbag, beacon, shovel, probe type products, safety, it's no coincidence that safety is one of our three pillars because it is so core to human survival and belonging and fulfillment. So yeah, that's a good call out.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:29:23): So when we first connected, you mentioned that you guys don't call your marketing department a marketing department. You mentioned that I believe you call it a brand and consumer department. What's the reason behind that and how does that connect to this conversation?", "token_estimate": 343, "speakers": ["Nicole Handel", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:28:05", "end_timestamp": "00:29:23", "podcast_index": 85, "episode_name": "Ep 85: Nicole Handel | Consumer Psychology and Decision Making | MAMMUT", "slug": "ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Nicole Handel, the Director of Marketing at MAMMUT joins me to discuss psychology and the consumer's decision-making process.", "podcast_length": "44:107"}, {"id": "d1d990a4-5462-43a8-ba60-68c52b60af12", "episode_id": "a7fea902-91ab-4add-9e95-c6224f4d3d0c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487b", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Nicole Handel (00:29:38): Yeah, so I think marketing takes into account ad campaigns, assets, content, copy. Those things are important, but for us, when we think about what we want our marketing department to do and how we can serve the brand, the consumer is really at the core of all of that. Everything we do, our three pillars are safety, sustainability, and DEI, the consumer's at the core of all of that, safety is to keep the consumer safe. DEI is to bring every consumer in and make everyone feel welcome. And sustainability is to make sure that there is a world left for all of us, the consumer to be a part of and to enjoy over time. So when we think about what brand and consumer means, brand is kind of like all of those aspects of a traditional marketing department, and consumer is the consumer. So our department, I think our department probably has our hands in more areas than a standard marketing department. Meaning the marketing department consists of avalanche safety, education, trainings, clinics, et cetera, consumer service, lots of different elements that really relate directly back to the consumer, not just to marketing with a capital M necessarily.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:31:01): Gotcha. So you see it going beyond the kind of conventional boundaries?\n\nNicole Handel (00:31:07): Yeah, absolutely. It's not just a flashy title meant to confuse people. We literally do a lot of work that directly relates to the consumer.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:31:17): What are some examples of how you guys might go beyond, above and beyond in that instance?", "token_estimate": 391, "speakers": ["Nicole Handel", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:29:38", "end_timestamp": "00:31:17", "podcast_index": 85, "episode_name": "Ep 85: Nicole Handel | Consumer Psychology and Decision Making | MAMMUT", "slug": "ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Nicole Handel, the Director of Marketing at MAMMUT joins me to discuss psychology and the consumer's decision-making process.", "podcast_length": "44:107"}, {"id": "5c6e1c79-ca04-46f0-87ee-10923e480791", "episode_id": "a7fea902-91ab-4add-9e95-c6224f4d3d0c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487b", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Nicole Handel (00:31:24): Well, we're working on some mentorship programs right now. So we have a lot of different partners who are frankly the experts in these fields of mentorship and outdoor safety. And so we're working on partnering with some of those partners to create, or I guess to kind of lessen that gap between the indoor and the outdoor or between the beginner and the expert and the points of accessibility. So both gym to Craig for climbing, and then a skiing mentorship program as well. And that's through the brand and consumer department. We've also had a bunch of different projects that I'll share about when they're further along, but basically one of our biggest goals in the brand and consumer department is finding ways to offer resources to the consumer. So we are really passionate about not just being a brand or a company or a business who creates products and sells them, but how can we do more and how can we be more? And the root of that, especially as a safety brand who specializes in technical outdoor apparel and gear, is safety. So we're working on a lot of resources right now that specifically speak to safety and education in the outdoors and bringing that to the consumer, which is not just a safety story, but also a DEI story because we know that so many people feel excluded and that there are barriers to entry. And if we can play our part in minimizing those barriers, we plan to,\n\nCole Heilborn (00:33:01): Why do we post those epic mountain get out there photos though?", "token_estimate": 379, "speakers": ["Nicole Handel", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:31:24", "end_timestamp": "00:33:01", "podcast_index": 85, "episode_name": "Ep 85: Nicole Handel | Consumer Psychology and Decision Making | MAMMUT", "slug": "ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Nicole Handel, the Director of Marketing at MAMMUT joins me to discuss psychology and the consumer's decision-making process.", "podcast_length": "44:107"}, {"id": "c052b35f-25ac-4347-bf48-a52bd4e2e97b", "episode_id": "a7fea902-91ab-4add-9e95-c6224f4d3d0c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487b", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Nicole Handel (00:33:08): Yeah, it's this kind of, I think, well, there are a few technical answers and then there are some more interesting psychological ones. The technical answer is Instagram has made everybody feel like they need to do everything and be everything. And when I say be everything, I mean it's not enough to just be, for example, I dunno, a small business owner, you have to be a photographer, a videographer, you have to follow trends, you have to be an algorithm expert. And so I think people share that kind of thing because there's this pressure to always be on and to always be engaging. And if you don't have anything particularly engaging to say or to show, you just show something or anything because there's this desperation to keep up. And then there's also the wow factor, big beautiful mountain shots. People love that we all double tap for that kind of thing. So I think that's an easy showstopper for people.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:34:07): So expand on, so you guys realize this, you realize that maybe this instance considering human behavior, this isn't what your audience needs. How are you trying to change that? Or in what ways are you seeing that being changed?", "token_estimate": 299, "speakers": ["Nicole Handel", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:33:08", "end_timestamp": "00:34:07", "podcast_index": 85, "episode_name": "Ep 85: Nicole Handel | Consumer Psychology and Decision Making | MAMMUT", "slug": "ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Nicole Handel, the Director of Marketing at MAMMUT joins me to discuss psychology and the consumer's decision-making process.", "podcast_length": "44:107"}, {"id": "55acb7f7-44e1-4df8-8d88-82282b505f45", "episode_id": "a7fea902-91ab-4add-9e95-c6224f4d3d0c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487b", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Nicole Handel (00:34:23): Yeah, so I mean the most basic way that we're trying to change that is just not feeding into that kind of empty content void. Nora who's on my brand and consumer team, runs our social media. And the conversations we have pretty often about social media are related to, like I said before, what can we share that's meaningful? So we share a lot of our partners content, like we work with the American Mountain Guide Association, we work with Airy, we work with pow. They often have important things to say, and we feel like if we don't have anything more important to say than them, we should be sharing what they're saying. We also feature our athletes and ambassadors and their insights. They all have such a diverse range of experiences and knowledge to share. So amplifying that is another way that we kind of get past the posts that are kind lacking in depth. (00:35:28): And of course, we share when we have a promotion on our website or we share when we have something going on as more of a notification. But we have really tried to shift to mostly offering resources on our social media and also sharing a more diverse set of faces. We are really, really proud of. Recently we've received some dms and some comments from people saying, Hey, I wasn't familiar with your brand. I found it through whatever X, Y, or Z, and I'm so happy to see so many women or so many whatever. Our feed is definitely coming further along in being a place of diversity and we're working on it. We are not experts, but we're consulting with experts and we're doing what we feel is our job to do in the industry, which is to not just show the same things that everyone is showing over and over and create this echo chamber, but to offer something more.", "token_estimate": 441, "speakers": ["Nicole Handel"], "start_timestamp": "00:34:23", "end_timestamp": "00:34:23", "podcast_index": 85, "episode_name": "Ep 85: Nicole Handel | Consumer Psychology and Decision Making | MAMMUT", "slug": "ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Nicole Handel, the Director of Marketing at MAMMUT joins me to discuss psychology and the consumer's decision-making process.", "podcast_length": "44:107"}, {"id": "e365ae33-6303-42aa-bb17-1ac9467d729c", "episode_id": "a7fea902-91ab-4add-9e95-c6224f4d3d0c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487b", "chunk_index": 19, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:36:30): I got to imagine one of the reasons why the Epic Mountain photo gets shared so many times is because I got to imagine at some point you just run out of things to say, and so because you feel like you need to say something just to stay top of mind or feeding the algorithm or whatever, you throw something out there. Are you trying to only say things when you need to say them? Or I guess, what's your thoughts on the algorithm portion of it all?\n\nNicole Handel (00:36:59): Yeah, we had a really, I think it was a two hour meeting several months back about social media. And we first were looking at it from this stereotypical approach that I think most brands look at their social media strategy through where we were like, what are we going to post on Monday? What are we going to post on Tuesday? What are we going to post on Wednesday? Because everybody who uses social media at any professional level knows that you do have to feed the algorithm. But like I said, when we took a step back and started asking ourselves, why are we sharing this mountain photo? We started to develop a strategy that was focused more around making sure that everything we shared offered some value and things slip through the cracks. Especially like I said earlier, if there's a photo from one of our athletes or if even a social media user, like a mam, consumer shares a photo with us and it's just super epic and we really want to share it. (00:38:02): We still do that from time to time. But I would say what has shifted is our mindset and our approach to social media and feeling less like we need to share at any cost and feeling more like we have all of these great things to share. How does that fit in? When will we share it, et cetera. Because really, I mean, we're a pretty talkative crew and we have a lot of opinions and thoughts, and we have tons of athletes on our team and we work with so many incredible partners. So really I think there's always something to share, and it doesn't have to be from us. It can be sharing something that a partner has shared that we think is important. So like I said, we're really moving away from the approach of feeling like we need to share just to share, and we're finding better ways to share.", "token_estimate": 564, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Nicole Handel"], "start_timestamp": "00:36:30", "end_timestamp": "00:36:59", "podcast_index": 85, "episode_name": "Ep 85: Nicole Handel | Consumer Psychology and Decision Making | MAMMUT", "slug": "ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Nicole Handel, the Director of Marketing at MAMMUT joins me to discuss psychology and the consumer's decision-making process.", "podcast_length": "44:107"}, {"id": "5ebab2f8-5cc8-490c-8940-7485421594ef", "episode_id": "a7fea902-91ab-4add-9e95-c6224f4d3d0c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487b", "chunk_index": 20, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:38:47): Interesting. Would you see negative results from doing that only in the number side of social media?\n\nNicole Handel (00:38:55): We haven't yet.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:38:56): You haven't yet? Interesting.\n\nNicole Handel (00:38:57): Yeah, and here's my hypothesis. Even if you're an algorithm expert, I don't think anybody really knows the exact science. It's guesswork and because the algorithm is scientific, but human behavior, there's more gray area. So my best guess at why we are seeing more engagement is because what we are sharing is more engaging. So the mountain shot does get a lot of likes, but the not tutorials or the right now today is giving Tuesday, and we're sharing a Giving Tuesday promotion where 15% of our e-comm sales support the AGA's Bipoc scholarship fund, that kind of thing. That's real. That's a real thing, and that is naturally more engaging. So our numbers have not suffered from this new approach probably because what we're doing is all more relatable to humanity than this sort of shallow pretty object.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:40:01): Sure. Yeah, no, well said. Continuing with this idea of human behavior and needs, what are some other applications beyond social media that you guys are reexamining or reinventing the ways that you have done things?", "token_estimate": 326, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Nicole Handel"], "start_timestamp": "00:38:47", "end_timestamp": "00:40:01", "podcast_index": 85, "episode_name": "Ep 85: Nicole Handel | Consumer Psychology and Decision Making | MAMMUT", "slug": "ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Nicole Handel, the Director of Marketing at MAMMUT joins me to discuss psychology and the consumer's decision-making process.", "podcast_length": "44:107"}, {"id": "f23e725b-aac4-42c9-a743-760d48dcc608", "episode_id": "a7fea902-91ab-4add-9e95-c6224f4d3d0c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487b", "chunk_index": 21, "content": "Nicole Handel (00:40:18): Yeah, I mean, we talk about sustainability and I would say here we have a ton of really, really strong initiatives that we're working on currently, and human behavior is a part of sustainability because the conversation that I think a lot of brands who feel like they have a responsibility to the outdoors are having is like, how do we get people to care and what can we do as a brand and what is our responsibility as a brand? We can adjust our practices, we can donate to organizations who are doing good for the environment. For example, we're partnered with POW at the global level. That's one small thing that we can do, but when we think about the big picture, which as I said is how do we get the consumer, how do we get humans to care about sustainability? That's a more challenging conversation because motivation is nuanced. So those are conversations we're having, but I don't think there are any clear answers, just some hypotheses.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:41:27): What do you mean motivation is nuanced? What does that mean?\n\nNicole Handel (00:41:31): I think I touched on this earlier. If we're motivated to do something, understanding why isn't always clear. A thought that I've had for years is I'm motivated to wake up early and run. Is that because it makes me feel good? Yes. Is that because I like identifying as somebody who is motivated to wake up early and run? Yes. Is it because I have dogs who need exercise? Yes. Those things are all my motivation. Those are my motives for doing that. But I think when we try to find a singular motivation, like a true core motivation, I think that's more challenging. There's a lot of gray area there.", "token_estimate": 421, "speakers": ["Nicole Handel", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:40:18", "end_timestamp": "00:41:31", "podcast_index": 85, "episode_name": "Ep 85: Nicole Handel | Consumer Psychology and Decision Making | MAMMUT", "slug": "ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Nicole Handel, the Director of Marketing at MAMMUT joins me to discuss psychology and the consumer's decision-making process.", "podcast_length": "44:107"}, {"id": "ed40870b-fe5b-4576-a0b2-7ebe06eacd12", "episode_id": "a7fea902-91ab-4add-9e95-c6224f4d3d0c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487b", "chunk_index": 22, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:42:20): So you did mention this earlier, but I want to come back to it. This idea that maybe one of the reasons why you run is because you like the idea of being someone who gets up early and runs. I've always heard that the why is the most important piece of how you do something or why you do something, but if the why is inauthentic, it seems kind of shallow, is that not, I don't know if problem is the right word, but that just doesn't seem as legit as I just like to get up and run because it feels good.\n\nNicole Handel (00:42:56): I think about this a lot, and I'm not shy about being very honest about the thoughts that come to mind. I think if we're all honest with ourselves, there's plenty of things that it would be easy for me to say I exclusively run because it makes me feel good. And frankly, that would be a lie. And I think anybody who runs skis, bikes, who does anything, I think anybody would be lying if they said that they could pinpoint this one clear singular motive. And I don't think that it's problematic because what's wrong with that is that hurting anybody. And like I said, motivation is nuanced. It does make me feel good. It is good for my dogs. It is a way for me to bond with others and form deep human relationships because usually you run with someone and that's one-on-one time that you get to spend with another human. It's pretty rare nowadays to have that concentrated alone time with someone. So there's lots of reasons, but I don't think it's harmful. I think it's just realistic to identify that among the reasons why we do something. Some of them are maybe selfish, some of them are maybe performative, but if we're doing something good in this example running, it's good for my body, it's good for my mental health, then the motivations can be nuanced and across the board of in quality.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:44:31): Because the outcome is ultimately a positive thing for you or whomever you're with, is that right?", "token_estimate": 495, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Nicole Handel"], "start_timestamp": "00:42:20", "end_timestamp": "00:44:31", "podcast_index": 85, "episode_name": "Ep 85: Nicole Handel | Consumer Psychology and Decision Making | MAMMUT", "slug": "ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Nicole Handel, the Director of Marketing at MAMMUT joins me to discuss psychology and the consumer's decision-making process.", "podcast_length": "44:107"}, {"id": "d877b893-0685-4b83-b813-5eee8a66243f", "episode_id": "a7fea902-91ab-4add-9e95-c6224f4d3d0c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487b", "chunk_index": 23, "content": "Nicole Handel (00:44:38): Right. Yeah. And I'm not saying that outcome is the only thing that matters in any situation ever. Motivation is important in a lot of conversations, but this one feels pretty harmless. Same thing like I mentioned earlier, with donating to a charitable cause. Whether or not we do that because we genuinely care about others or if, because us caring about others is just an expression of ego. Does it matter if we're doing good? Does it matter? Some people would say yes, and in some contexts I would agree yes, but I think this is really more subconscious. I don't know that most people would identify one way or the other. If they're doing something good, they are a good person, and it doesn't matter if super subconsciously, that's an ego thing. Ego is a part of good acts and good things, and so I don't think that that's necessarily harmful.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:45:40): Sure. Yeah. Well, to expand on, I think what you just mentioned, this idea of if you're donating to a nonprofit and if you're doing it because you like to have, for instance, the 1% for the planet logo on your website, are you doing it because you really like the logo or are you doing it because you genuinely believe in the act of giving and supporting? Maybe it isn't one or the other. It's probably\n\nNicole Handel (00:46:04): Right. It's usually both. To be clear, this is not me calling out people who do things like that and saying they're doing it for selfish reasons. My point is more that human behavior is complex and so many people do good things and also have other subconscious motivations, and that's okay. And I think that's just a reality that we could all recognize.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:46:30): Is that why you enjoy it? Or what about it? You could just spin circles around yourself.", "token_estimate": 450, "speakers": ["Nicole Handel", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:44:38", "end_timestamp": "00:46:30", "podcast_index": 85, "episode_name": "Ep 85: Nicole Handel | Consumer Psychology and Decision Making | MAMMUT", "slug": "ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Nicole Handel, the Director of Marketing at MAMMUT joins me to discuss psychology and the consumer's decision-making process.", "podcast_length": "44:107"}, {"id": "858ba8cf-5c0c-4d70-a6d2-bc362a510edf", "episode_id": "a7fea902-91ab-4add-9e95-c6224f4d3d0c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487b", "chunk_index": 24, "content": "Nicole Handel (00:46:36): And I do. I certainly do. I have on this call in our conversation. No, that is why I love it. I don't think you can escape human behavior or the concepts of motivation and wants and needs. None of us can escape that, and that's daunting for some people, or it's exciting and encouraging for some, and I get really excited by that. I think that's kind of what keeps me really interested in marketing, because systems and processes and technology, those things all change and specific motivations change, but the concept of human behavior, that's always there. That's always going to be there until we're replaced by robots who are incapable of human emotion, which probably will never happen because if humans are creating robots, they'll have to have some human-like aspects, but it's always going to be there. So that's what I love about it.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:47:35): Is there ever a point where marketing and psychology don't intersect? Is there a place where they should be separate or where they naturally fall apart from each other?\n\nNicole Handel (00:47:47): Oh, interesting question. I mean, I think I stand by the fact that psychology is in everything. I was going to say maybe in one of those stereotypical flashy email subject lines like attention open this, but that's psychology. We're appealing to a human's sense of urgency, or I think a lot of times it's not intentionally ingrained. I don't think psychology is always intentional in marketing, but I do think it's always there.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:48:27): Could we use the psychology? I feel like what this conversation is helping me realize is knowing these tools, they're really tools. You can encourage someone to do something by speaking a language that they understand or speaks to 'em. I feel like we could all come up with examples where we feel like we've been prodded in the wrong direction. And that kind of gets back to your early comment about this feels kind of slimy and gross, but there's also ways to use these tools in a positive way. What are some other positive examples or tactics that you feel like could be used to move good initiatives or good messages out?", "token_estimate": 543, "speakers": ["Nicole Handel", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:46:36", "end_timestamp": "00:48:27", "podcast_index": 85, "episode_name": "Ep 85: Nicole Handel | Consumer Psychology and Decision Making | MAMMUT", "slug": "ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Nicole Handel, the Director of Marketing at MAMMUT joins me to discuss psychology and the consumer's decision-making process.", "podcast_length": "44:107"}, {"id": "5e5a810a-d939-4c67-97dd-ac34fcc58387", "episode_id": "a7fea902-91ab-4add-9e95-c6224f4d3d0c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487b", "chunk_index": 25, "content": "Nicole Handel (00:49:13): Yeah. Well, again, not to oversimplify, but not even a tactic, but a framework or a perspective would be what do people want to see? And in a non slimy way, I don't mean what do people want to see? Diet culture mean? What do people want? Do people want to feel knowledgeable? Do people want to feel safe? Do people want a cause to support? And we can help direct that. What is it that people are looking for? And that's the most basic question in marketing, is what do consumers want? But if you can shape that in a way that's beneficial mutually to the brand and the consumer, sure, we want consumers to love our brand and our products, but we also want consumers to love what we do and how we treat them and what we stand for. So I think I don't have a big flashy secret to what you're asking, but the very simple answer is just asking yourself what consumers want. That's the tactic, that's the tip or trick. And it's really quite simple in theory. It's of course more complex when you get down to it because you can identify what consumers want and not know how to provide it or not know how to give it or be it or do it, but it's not too hard to figure out what people want.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:50:44): Yeah, I guess that is really simple, but also it's kind of at the root of everything.\n\nNicole Handel (00:50:48): Yeah, yeah. All of this stuff is the kind of just mind blowing, super big, but super specific sort of topics. It's everything. And it's also really tiny and specific. So yeah, it's definitely a rabbit hole.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:51:03): How do the needs of the consumer change over time?", "token_estimate": 408, "speakers": ["Nicole Handel", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:49:13", "end_timestamp": "00:51:03", "podcast_index": 85, "episode_name": "Ep 85: Nicole Handel | Consumer Psychology and Decision Making | MAMMUT", "slug": "ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Nicole Handel, the Director of Marketing at MAMMUT joins me to discuss psychology and the consumer's decision-making process.", "podcast_length": "44:107"}, {"id": "8585b968-ad26-44c7-9482-3229f56c635b", "episode_id": "a7fea902-91ab-4add-9e95-c6224f4d3d0c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487b", "chunk_index": 26, "content": "Nicole Handel (00:51:06): Oh, well, I mean, I think COVID is a great example of something big, disruptive and unexpected. One really clear example would be we produced masks when COVID first happened, and so many brands who had the ability and the materials and the processes in place to do that, produced masks at the start of COVID, that's very specific, but consumer needs are always going to change. The world is, this sounds really obvious to say, but the world is constantly evolving and brands have to as well. So I think just revisiting that question constantly, what's needed now is the way to work with rather than against that ever changing kind of culture.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:51:59): So if sustainability and DEI are topics that the audience or the consumers need to hear about, we need to be talking about now. If you had to guess in five years from now, will those be similar issues that are going to be as prevalent or will there be a new set of needs that need to be addressed? Because these ones are not, we haven't figured it out, but we've talked about it.", "token_estimate": 269, "speakers": ["Nicole Handel", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:51:06", "end_timestamp": "00:51:59", "podcast_index": 85, "episode_name": "Ep 85: Nicole Handel | Consumer Psychology and Decision Making | MAMMUT", "slug": "ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Nicole Handel, the Director of Marketing at MAMMUT joins me to discuss psychology and the consumer's decision-making process.", "podcast_length": "44:107"}, {"id": "0bc3db2d-3923-4b33-abee-5a1d6ee9a961", "episode_id": "a7fea902-91ab-4add-9e95-c6224f4d3d0c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487b", "chunk_index": 27, "content": "Nicole Handel (00:52:24): Well, I feel two ways. My initial response was like, I hope we're still talking about this in five years, but my secondary response is, I hope we don't have to talk about this in five years. I hope that we don't have to tell people that diversity, equity, and inclusion should be at the core of what they're doing and how they're behaving. Sustainability, I think will always be there, though it's no secret that the climate isn't getting better and the earth isn't getting stronger, I think sustainability will be around forever. But to get to the root of what I think you're asking is will there be a new hot topic in five years? Of course, I choose not to look at these things as on-trend topics because in the case of sustainability and DEI, this is not a hot topic. These are issues that have been around for a long time. And what's changed is that we're finally being held accountable for the damage we've done to people and to the environment. So I'm sure there are lots more things for us to be held accountable for. So I'm sure we'll see more in the next five years, but I hope that we've made some progress on those things before that happens.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:53:34): Sure. Yeah. And continue on people's reactions to if you're posting more content that's hopefully solving a problem or solving a need. What's the feedback been?", "token_estimate": 342, "speakers": ["Nicole Handel", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:52:24", "end_timestamp": "00:53:34", "podcast_index": 85, "episode_name": "Ep 85: Nicole Handel | Consumer Psychology and Decision Making | MAMMUT", "slug": "ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Nicole Handel, the Director of Marketing at MAMMUT joins me to discuss psychology and the consumer's decision-making process.", "podcast_length": "44:107"}, {"id": "94e42797-ffe2-4c95-928b-deebba57313d", "episode_id": "a7fea902-91ab-4add-9e95-c6224f4d3d0c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487b", "chunk_index": 28, "content": "Nicole Handel (00:53:48): Yeah, I mean, when you offer resources, I think like you're putting yourself out there. We've certainly received some feedback on not tying videos by folks saying, that's not how I would tie that knot. And that's fine. We don't need everybody to feel the same way or to think the same way. So there's been some of that feedback, but we've also received a lot of feedback from people saying, Hey, I would not have been comfortable asking somebody to show me how to tie a figure eight knot, and it's really cool to see it here on your feed. So we've had a lot of positive feedback and we all feel really good about what we're doing. We're certainly open to more constructive feedback on what we could do better. We definitely encourage that. But so far it seems like people are interested in seeing some different things on their feed.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:54:41): And I suppose in a way, a company is no different than an individual.\n\nNicole Handel (00:54:46): Sure. It's a business. Yeah. This is something again, that at Mammo, we're not really shy about. We are a business. We do have to sell things, but our approach is how can we do good and impact the environment minimally while we operate? Because that's any business who, if you're a business and you're not a nonprofit, your motivation has to be to stay afloat. And the way that I see it is that if the more successful we can be at mout, the more good we can do. So really there's no shame in being in business to be in business, but what are you doing along the way? How are you minimizing impact? What are you giving back? Who are you bringing in? That has to be part of the conversation for me as well.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:55:37): As you guys are on this path trying to do good, how do you bring the consumer along? How do you educate the consumer about those issues, but give them ownership and use human behavior to help influence their behavior?", "token_estimate": 484, "speakers": ["Nicole Handel", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:53:48", "end_timestamp": "00:55:37", "podcast_index": 85, "episode_name": "Ep 85: Nicole Handel | Consumer Psychology and Decision Making | MAMMUT", "slug": "ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Nicole Handel, the Director of Marketing at MAMMUT joins me to discuss psychology and the consumer's decision-making process.", "podcast_length": "44:107"}, {"id": "84a74af2-0a84-415d-8741-ccaa1f59c45b", "episode_id": "a7fea902-91ab-4add-9e95-c6224f4d3d0c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487b", "chunk_index": 29, "content": "Nicole Handel (00:55:53): Yeah. I dunno if I'll answer this as clearly as what you're asking, but like I mentioned earlier, a mentorship program, that's a great example of how do we bring the consumer in and make sure that they're involved in person events or in in-person activations like Jim to Craig mentorship program type concept. That's a very explicit, obvious way of bringing the consumer along. And again, educational resources, you can't bring along somebody who doesn't think that they belong or doesn't think that they have the knowledge offering those resources. And again, we have a lot of plans and ideas, and not all of them are activated on yet. We could be doing more, we could always be doing more, but we really feel like education is kind of the first barrier to entry that prevent a lot of people from getting into the outdoor world in any sport. (00:56:56): So I think education is at the core of bringing the consumer in, and that's what we hope to fix. I mean, I think for me, what makes sense, and I have not been in this industry for as long as so many people who are in my role or in the industry alongside of me. But for me, my biggest learning from being in this role for the time that I have so far is that what we should all be thinking of, like I said earlier, is what does the consumer need? What's missing? What is there too much of? And what is there room for? And how can we be of service? So in a more traditional departmentalized view of a brand, the sales team is there to sell and the marketing team is there to help them sell. But for us, like I said, with brand and consumer being our kind of vision of the marketing team, we get to think of it as how can we serve the consumer? And we can do that through sales as well. And really every department can come into play there. But that's the question that I think would do all of us well to think about and to consider in any kind of marketing role is what can we do for the consumer? How can we help? And like I said, we haven't figured it all out yet, but that's the question that's shaping most of our actions and our ideas.", "token_estimate": 530, "speakers": ["Nicole Handel"], "start_timestamp": "00:55:53", "end_timestamp": "00:55:53", "podcast_index": 85, "episode_name": "Ep 85: Nicole Handel | Consumer Psychology and Decision Making | MAMMUT", "slug": "ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Nicole Handel, the Director of Marketing at MAMMUT joins me to discuss psychology and the consumer's decision-making process.", "podcast_length": "44:107"}, {"id": "39097039-b894-40a5-af3e-fc000f135e1c", "episode_id": "a7fea902-91ab-4add-9e95-c6224f4d3d0c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487b", "chunk_index": 30, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:58:32): Yeah. So we've kind of danced around a little bit. Is there any topic that you don't feel like we have covered that you've throw something out there about?\n\nNicole Handel (00:58:41): No. I mean, the only topic that I came into this fully prepared to discuss was what we talked about, which is how do you get someone to care about something? That's the only thing we didn't discuss. But I don't have any, there's nothing that I needed to discuss. I think this has been great.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:58:58): So you've talked about a number of different initiatives that OO has been working on, but I think maybe the thing that you have to figure out how to do in the first place is to get people to care about those issues that you guys are supporting and bring people into them. So from a broad perspective, how do you get people to care about these issues that you guys care about?", "token_estimate": 226, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Nicole Handel"], "start_timestamp": "00:58:32", "end_timestamp": "00:58:58", "podcast_index": 85, "episode_name": "Ep 85: Nicole Handel | Consumer Psychology and Decision Making | MAMMUT", "slug": "ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Nicole Handel, the Director of Marketing at MAMMUT joins me to discuss psychology and the consumer's decision-making process.", "podcast_length": "44:107"}, {"id": "af5e8c45-01a9-4e79-beab-d142428e8f04", "episode_id": "a7fea902-91ab-4add-9e95-c6224f4d3d0c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487b", "chunk_index": 31, "content": "Nicole Handel (00:59:20): Yeah, so my favorite example when I talk about this because it comes up frequently, is that my partner is a fly fishing guide. And I grew up hunting, but for food, very, very utilitarian, upstate New York, like family tradition kind of thing. It was not, nothing about, it was for sport, and we never looked at it as a sport. My partner is a catch and release fly fishing guide. And when we met, the concept frankly felt gross to me because I kept thinking about why are we doing this? Why does a human have the right to put a fish through stress for our own enjoyment and our own sport? And my partner, Dominic, isn't like that kind of person. We stereotype what sport fishing or sport hunting people are like, but I learned that not everybody has the same motivations and there's actually a lot of good that can be done. (01:00:17): So in the conversations we've had about why catch and release fishing is or could be ethical, taking into account using ethical practices like how to handle the fish safely, et cetera. His position was that he is a conservationist. He volunteers for some organizations that put a lot of work into river conservation. And he grew up fly fishing. I didn't grow up fly fishing. I had no connection to rivers or bodies of water, really. I hadn't spent much time on them. So I didn't know anything about river conservation or the species that exist in those environments. I didn't know a single thing. And if you ask me, do you care about water conservation? Yes, but have I ever done anything for water conservation? Did I know a single thing about it? No. So what he explained to me was you, in order to get somebody to care about something, they have to have an emotional attachment, a specific experience. (01:01:16): So the reason why he cares about river conservation is because he grew up on a river. He grew up figuring out which species of fish are eating, which species of bugs, and at what time of year and at what time of day, and what is the water temperature, where they're most comfortable and can thrive. All of these factors that people don't realize go into fly fishing. There's so many things and it's really more about environment than anything else. And so he learned all of that growing up, fly fishing, and that gave him this love for the river and for the environments that exist around rivers that he couldn't have had if he didn't have those specific experiences. And now as a fly fishing guide, he teaches those environmental factors, those safe handling procedures, all of this conservation work to the people. And that gives people this very specific, very emotional, very personal attachment to a place or an environment. (01:02:16): And that's the only way that will care about something. If you've never been skiing or you live somewhere without snow, you probably don't care about glaciers, for example, because why should you have nothing to attach to that? It's abstract. It's like someone telling you that Saturn isn't doing so well right now. What does that mean to any of us? Unless you know what it means. So I think to bring this full circle for a brand, how do you get somebody to care about the environment? How do you get somebody to care about DEI? You show them why they should. So for us, this comes back to how do we literally take people out skiing? How do we show people how to climb outside after they've gone to a gym a couple of times? How can we be a driving force in exposing people to these environments and these things that we all love in order to get them to love them too? (01:03:11): And it sounds contradictory because, and it is nuanced. There are all these conversations about access and overpopulation, and can a certain location withstand impact from more people, but can a certain location withstand the impact of not having more people care about it? Undoubtedly, no. That's the bottom line. So for me, the concept is getting people to the care has to be personal, has to be emotional and has to have connection. So the hard part then is figuring out how do we create that connection? How do we facilitate more? Because really that's all we can be as a facilitator. So that's on the to-do list right now. That's the current big picture project amongst some others. But like I said, we don't have all the answers, but we are asking questions and I think that's a good start.", "token_estimate": 1099, "speakers": ["Nicole Handel"], "start_timestamp": "00:59:20", "end_timestamp": "00:59:20", "podcast_index": 85, "episode_name": "Ep 85: Nicole Handel | Consumer Psychology and Decision Making | MAMMUT", "slug": "ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Nicole Handel, the Director of Marketing at MAMMUT joins me to discuss psychology and the consumer's decision-making process.", "podcast_length": "44:107"}, {"id": "6242a165-297e-45b8-924c-609b3f353ef3", "episode_id": "a7fea902-91ab-4add-9e95-c6224f4d3d0c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487b", "chunk_index": 32, "content": "Cole Heilborn (01:04:08): Well, it sounds like you have to, like you said, you have to give them the experience you have to, and in doing so, you're hopefully giving them the need for themselves to see the places they care about protected in the way they hope they're protected. Well, that's awesome. Can you elaborate on how you're trying to break that down into actionable steps?\n\nNicole Handel (01:04:31): Yeah, so like I said earlier, the first step is literally getting people outside. And so we are working with some of our partners on programs that do just that. Like I've said a couple times, we're not experts in that, but we have partners who are, and so working with people who best know how to get others outside and supporting with gear, with financial support, amplifying their messages on our channels, that kind of thing. So step one is getting people outside step two or maybe one B is showing them how to do that safely. If you don't know how to tie a knot, you probably are going to be pretty intimidated to go climb outside. Not to oversimplify, but that's kind of the first action item. And I think that's really the only action item that we are at the phase of talking about, because the next action item is doing that over and over and over again. (01:05:32): We can get someone outside, but how do we make sure that they continue to pursue an interest or how do we support them continuing to pursue that interest to create that emotional attachment? And it's, again, it's not a scheme like, oh, how can we manipulate people into caring about the environment? But it's more like we know what we feel on our brand and consumer team. We are all passionate outdoors people. We all do different things. We have anglers, mountain bikers, skiers, climbers. We have people in every sport. And so the question is how do we show people what we're so passionate about? How do we share that? How do we make that love contagious? And that's probably easier said than done, but step one is just get people outside and that's what we're working on.", "token_estimate": 515, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Nicole Handel"], "start_timestamp": "01:04:08", "end_timestamp": "01:04:31", "podcast_index": 85, "episode_name": "Ep 85: Nicole Handel | Consumer Psychology and Decision Making | MAMMUT", "slug": "ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Nicole Handel, the Director of Marketing at MAMMUT joins me to discuss psychology and the consumer's decision-making process.", "podcast_length": "44:107"}, {"id": "481d8e65-1c3f-42d9-bb73-6a01c14613b4", "episode_id": "a7fea902-91ab-4add-9e95-c6224f4d3d0c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c487b", "chunk_index": 33, "content": "Cole Heilborn (01:06:19): Yeah. Awesome. Well, I'm sure that seems like a giant to-do list to giant tasks to overcome, but I think as you've mentioned, humans are pretty repetitive and we same can make decisions over and over. So it sounds like you guys are on the right track trying to implement some of those things, I guess with the understanding that you have about how humans work.\n\nNicole Handel (01:06:42): Yeah. Thank you. We hope so.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:06:44): We're running up against the end of our time for today, but Nicole, if you, I dunno, do you have any other lasting thoughts? Anything else you want to end the show with?\n\nNicole Handel (01:06:53): No, I think we covered lots of stuff. Probably more stuff than I would normally talk about in this amount of time, so I think we're good.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:07:02): Cool. Well, if people want to follow along with you personally with Mamo or any of the awesome initiatives you guys have going on, where can people go to connect?\n\nNicole Handel (01:07:12): Yeah, for Mamu on Instagram, we are at Mamu na, that's for North America. And we're also@www.mamu.com and that's M-A-M-M-U-T. And personally, I'm at Nicole Handel, that's H-A-N-D-E-L on Instagram. And if you like dogs, that's all you need. You'll be a fan.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:07:37): Sounds good. Well, Nicole, I hope you have a great rest of your day. Thanks again for sharing this last hour with us and I'm looking forward to seeing some of the work that you guys are working on behind the scenes come out into the world.\n\nNicole Handel (01:07:49): Cool. Thanks for having me, Cole. Appreciate it.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:07:52): Alright, take care.\n\nNicole Handel (01:07:53): You too.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:07:56): Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learn something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 485, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Nicole Handel"], "start_timestamp": "01:06:19", "end_timestamp": "01:07:56", "podcast_index": 85, "episode_name": "Ep 85: Nicole Handel | Consumer Psychology and Decision Making | MAMMUT", "slug": "ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-85-nicole-handel-consumer-psychology-and-decision-making-mammut", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Nicole Handel, the Director of Marketing at MAMMUT joins me to discuss psychology and the consumer's decision-making process.", "podcast_length": "44:107"}, {"id": "2c98cb32-8375-4d44-b308-1c21a28fc685", "episode_id": "ee121690-42a3-4ed7-8bba-6415092b2a63", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c482a", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:00): Welcome to Brand Story. My name is Cole Hilborn and I'm going to be your host Today Brand story is all about the marketing professionals who are working in the industry today. The landscape is constantly changing and evolving, takes really great smart people to run a successful brand, and that's what this podcast is all about. It's all about the people behind the brands we interact with, the challenges they face, as well as their personal journey. So if you're interested in learning more about what it takes to run a top brand, I hope you'll love this podcast and find something educational about it. Enjoy today's episode. Welcome to Brand Story, the podcast where we interview marketing professionals who are designing and creating some really amazing brands out there. My name is Cole Hilborn and I'm sitting down today with Charlie Loeser, the Vice President of Digital Media at Backbone Media. Charlie, how are you? Doing\n\nCharlie Lozner (00:48): Great. Yeah, excited for a good Colorado weekend coming up.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:53): Yeah. You're over in Carbondale, is that right?\n\nCharlie Lozner (00:56): Yeah, Carbondale, Colorado. It's a real small mountain town on the Western Slope. We're like three hours west of Denver and about 30 minutes down the valley from Aspen.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:06): Perfect. It sounds like you guys have a lot of great outdoor opportunities in that neck of the woods.\n\nCharlie Lozner (01:12): Yeah, it's sort of where two rivers come together, the Crystal River and the Roaring Fork River, and so you can kind of go up either River Valley into two great and very different worlds up the Roaring Fork. You head towards Aspen and everybody sort of knows what Aspen's like, but world-class skiing and mountain biking and all sorts of outdoor recreation up that way. And then you can head up the crystal towards the little town of Marble, which only has about 200 people and tons of fun stuff to do back there too. Give back country skiing. No real mountain biking up that way, but yeah, hiking, fishing, backpacking.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:49): What do you do at Backbone? Tell us a little bit about the company you work for.", "token_estimate": 546, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Charlie Lozner"], "start_timestamp": "00:00", "end_timestamp": "01:49", "podcast_index": 8, "episode_name": "Ep 8: Charlie Lozner | Climate Change and Personal Responsibility in the Outdoor Industry | Backbone Medi‪a‬", "slug": "ep-8-charlie-lozner-climate-change-and-personal-responsibility-in-the-outdoor-industry-backbone-media", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-8-charlie-lozner-climate-change-and-personal-responsibility-in-the-outdoor-industry-backbone-media", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "For Charlie, the work that gives him the most satisfaction is work that combats the effects of climate change. The outdoor industry has made strides in lobbying for Mother Earth but there is more work to be done.", "podcast_length": "44:30"}, {"id": "18a9414e-7668-442f-937d-ce9c1031716a", "episode_id": "ee121690-42a3-4ed7-8bba-6415092b2a63", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c482a", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Charlie Lozner (01:53): Yeah, so Backbone Media is an active lifestyle marketing agency. We've been around for, I think 23 years now. The agency first started when the founders Penn Newhart and Lisa Raleigh left Climbing magazine in 1997 to form an agency that was there to really help active lifestyle brands get their message out. So primarily we started with public relations, working with tiny little climbing brands and backcountry ski brands about the smallest back in the early nineties or mid nineties. Those were pretty small industries and small companies, but the agency has just grown successively over 23 years from that gestation. Now, we've worked with brands like Black Diamond Equipment for I think 22 of the 23 years. We've had some great opportunities with brands like Yeti Coolers and New Belgium Brewing, black Diamond, we mentioned Patagonia. So really working with some of the best brands in the outdoor and active lifestyle industry. (03:01): PR is still kind of what we do primarily, but we also have the other half of our business that maybe less fewer people know about is paid media. So it started with just buying print ads and digital ads and outdoor titles, but early on we got an opportunity with New Belgium brewing as that brewery was going national. So we really had to create the first paid media campaigns that were reaching a broader audience. It wasn't just going after climbers and backcountry skiers and stuff like that. It was really going after that craft beer audience. And we created campaigns, local campaigns in 50 states and helped take new Belgium out nationally. And so that really formalized what we were doing on the paid media side. So those are the two primary businesses. And then about five years ago, we really had to evolve what we were doing on the paid media and digital media side as more and more brands wanted to start to kind of communicate directly with their end consumer and launched their direct to consumer business alongside probably a wholesale business. (04:19): We had to just evolve and add services. So now we're a fully certified Google partner, we're a fully certified Facebook partner. We're buying, at this point, tens of millions of dollars with those two behemoths for better or worse, but it's a necessary service that we've got to offer in order to be competitive in the landscape. So now today we can offer a brand the opportunity to do pr. We can do brand media for them in the form of maybe it's an out-of-home campaign billboards in a local market to launch a store. Maybe we'll do something like Connected TV to help get a brand anthem out. That's something we did for Smart Wool. We actually helped Patagonia launch their first connected TV campaign last fall to get the Y recycled campaign out. It was a campaign about how important recycling is and creating a market for the raw material that comes out of the recycling process. (05:26): It's something that Patagonia has done super well, but then we can also run full funnel campaigns. So everything from creating awareness and getting a brand in front of a new audience all the way through to driving conversion on their website, which is critical today. Unfortunately, so many brick and mortar stores are closed right now. And even Amazon's prioritizing shipping essential goods over non-essential goods like whatever backpacks and climbing gear and stuff like that. So it's been critical for these brands to have a direct to consumer business in this time, and they've been able to, in some ways, whether the storm turning product into cash by selling directly. So yeah, the agency has a lot of different services. We're Colorado based, Carbondale is hometown, and where we've got about, I think 35 people or so, and then we've got another 25 or so down in Denver, (06:34): And we work with about I think 60 or 70 different clients, mostly in outdoor and a really broad definition of outdoor. So it's not just hiking, backpacking, skiing, climbing, mountain biking, stuff like that. But also we've got a really strong business in the hunting and fishing space, working with kind of best in class brands there. We do a lot of work in the tourism space, so working with primarily destinations like Breckenridge or Telluride or Estes Park, a few resorts here and there. And then we also really cool project we do is with Ski Canada, so it's sort of the tourism arm of Canada, and our job is to convince Americans to go north to go skiing. So that's pretty fun. And then we do some work in the craft beer, craft spirits, continuing to work with new Belgium brewing, but craft beer, craft spirits, and then natural food space as well.", "token_estimate": 1169, "speakers": ["Charlie Lozner"], "start_timestamp": "01:53", "end_timestamp": "01:53", "podcast_index": 8, "episode_name": "Ep 8: Charlie Lozner | Climate Change and Personal Responsibility in the Outdoor Industry | Backbone Medi‪a‬", "slug": "ep-8-charlie-lozner-climate-change-and-personal-responsibility-in-the-outdoor-industry-backbone-media", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-8-charlie-lozner-climate-change-and-personal-responsibility-in-the-outdoor-industry-backbone-media", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "For Charlie, the work that gives him the most satisfaction is work that combats the effects of climate change. The outdoor industry has made strides in lobbying for Mother Earth but there is more work to be done.", "podcast_length": "44:30"}, {"id": "dea85e61-1252-44ca-9a95-81a07d994bf4", "episode_id": "ee121690-42a3-4ed7-8bba-6415092b2a63", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c482a", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Cole Heilborn (07:38): Alright. Wow. That's a lot of stuff.\n\nCharlie Lozner (07:40): Yeah, it's a lot of stuff. It keeps us busy.\n\nCole Heilborn (07:43): Yeah, that's great. So as the vice president of digital media, what do you do? What do you oversee? What do your day-to-day look like?\n\nCharlie Lozner (07:52): Yeah, so no day is the same, so there's not really a day-to-day, but I oversee a team with one of my partners, Greg Williams, oversee a team of about 25 people on the media side. And the reason that team has grown that large is we don't outsource any of the work. So when we're buying Facebook ads or we're buying Google ads or we're buying YouTube ads, that's our team doing it. We don't farm it out. So it's a lot of growing and nurturing the team, teaching them what I know, but at this point they all know more than I do, and it's more how do we set up a culture of sharing that knowledge within the team, managing relationships with clients. On the media side, we've got about 35 or 40 clients, so trying to keep that all straight and help them be successful and then learn new things. (08:53): So landscape's evolving. Facebook and Google are constantly rolling out new features and new product offerings, and then you got to stay on top of a couple of years ago it was Snapchat and certainly their platform hasn't gone away and their advertising is evolving, but then you got to stay on top of the new stuff. Pinterest rolling out more shopping opportunities. Everybody's talking about TikTok now with COVID and staying at home and doing dance videos. And it's funny, we don't have brands that are trying to reach 13 to 18-year-old customer too often. Sometimes we do work with Colorado Mountain College, and maybe that'll be the first one that we do on Snapchat, or first one we do on TikTok because they're going after that high school student that's trying to think about where do I go for where do I go to school or should I be doing some evening school programs while I'm in high school or something like that. So yeah, it's fun. It keeps you on your toes just trying to stay on top of the landscape. It's changing so fast and each client's objectives are a little bit different, but there's common themes that really allow us to get good at it and share those learnings across clients.", "token_estimate": 578, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Charlie Lozner"], "start_timestamp": "07:38", "end_timestamp": "07:52", "podcast_index": 8, "episode_name": "Ep 8: Charlie Lozner | Climate Change and Personal Responsibility in the Outdoor Industry | Backbone Medi‪a‬", "slug": "ep-8-charlie-lozner-climate-change-and-personal-responsibility-in-the-outdoor-industry-backbone-media", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-8-charlie-lozner-climate-change-and-personal-responsibility-in-the-outdoor-industry-backbone-media", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "For Charlie, the work that gives him the most satisfaction is work that combats the effects of climate change. The outdoor industry has made strides in lobbying for Mother Earth but there is more work to be done.", "podcast_length": "44:30"}, {"id": "c5ef05da-f2a1-4392-acdc-e8466e559c67", "episode_id": "ee121690-42a3-4ed7-8bba-6415092b2a63", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c482a", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Cole Heilborn (10:14): Yeah. Are there any specific success stories for clients or campaigns that you've been a part of that, I dunno, that you still get excited about or like, oh man, that was awesome. Anything off the top of your head?\n\nCharlie Lozner (10:29): Yeah, definitely. There's a couple that come to mind. I'd say the one that I'm particularly proud of was helping Patagonia get the Why Recycled Campaign out. So it was a two part campaign where first we were getting a broad message about how important recycling is and creating a full supply chain, not just putting your plastic and cans out at the curb, but there has to be demand at the other side of that chain. And that's something that Patagonia has done really well is more and more of their fleece, their face fabrics, their nylons, their polyesters is coming from recycled base material. And so they're creating the demand so that what we put in the recycling bin doesn't just end up in the landfill. (11:20): So we teed up the message, and I think it was about a 14 or 15 minute long film, which is hard to capture people's attention online. They want to watch something that's 15 seconds long. And so we had to figure out how do we drive traffic to this 15 minute long film about recycling, not the most exciting thing in the world, but the film was very well done. And then I think our execution on driving traffic to it was also well done. And then that segued into more of a celebration video where Patagonia was, it was called Shell, yeah, instead of Hell Yeah, it was Shell and it was all about how they'd converted their entire hard shell product offering into recycled face fabrics and then Fair Trade Zone. And they were just celebrating that. I mean, first, I think probably first company of that size or scale that had the entire, that portion of their product line with recycled materials. So that was a really fun campaign. It had both a sort of cause and value based element, but it was also just a real challenge from a digital execution standpoint.\n\nCole Heilborn (12:27): Yeah.\n\nCharlie Lozner (12:28): Also, go ahead.", "token_estimate": 527, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Charlie Lozner"], "start_timestamp": "10:14", "end_timestamp": "12:28", "podcast_index": 8, "episode_name": "Ep 8: Charlie Lozner | Climate Change and Personal Responsibility in the Outdoor Industry | Backbone Medi‪a‬", "slug": "ep-8-charlie-lozner-climate-change-and-personal-responsibility-in-the-outdoor-industry-backbone-media", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-8-charlie-lozner-climate-change-and-personal-responsibility-in-the-outdoor-industry-backbone-media", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "For Charlie, the work that gives him the most satisfaction is work that combats the effects of climate change. The outdoor industry has made strides in lobbying for Mother Earth but there is more work to be done.", "podcast_length": "44:30"}, {"id": "5e6760f1-39c3-4f06-aef0-4cdd0fb3036c", "episode_id": "ee121690-42a3-4ed7-8bba-6415092b2a63", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c482a", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Cole Heilborn (12:30): Oh no, I was just going to clarify some questions on that. So that type of a campaign that has a very, I think I'm assuming for you, a very personal connection to it. Can you explain more? How did you feel personally being able to work on a campaign like that where you're working on something that's not just good for the brand, but good for the greater good the planet?\n\nCharlie Lozner (12:55): Yeah, so initially super nervous. Our first opportunity with Patagonia was a little more tactical and it was just going to be the Shelly Ya side of that campaign. And then we got brought in to do the brand side of it too. Shelly Ya was considered to be a product campaign and then the Why recycled was more of a brand campaign. But yeah, it was nerve wracking. Patagonia is held up as one of the best marketing companies in the world constantly when people are like, what brand's doing it well, people point to that brand. And so yeah, it's nerve wracking to come in there and they're asking backbone for our expertise. So it was initially nerve wracking, but then we realize that, oh, we do have expertise in this area, we know what we're talking about. We've run a lot of campaigns like this. We've got the relationships, we've got the expertise. So then it turned into some more, I think more confidence that the plan was a good one. And after that though, just yeah, proud. I mean, it's cool to do something with a brand like that and bring forth new ideas, but then have it all sort of ladder up to a bigger cause (14:23): And that organization certainly walks the talk. They really practice. It's not marketing, it's embedded in the values of that organization. So that's cool to see and definitely a campaign that I think we're all super proud of.\n\nCole Heilborn (14:45): You mentioned climate change, and I know that's something you're really passionate about and the outdoor industry is also very vocal about what is being done in the outdoor industry or even what are some of the brands that you guys support? What are they doing to work through the complicated issue of climate change?", "token_estimate": 529, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Charlie Lozner"], "start_timestamp": "12:30", "end_timestamp": "14:45", "podcast_index": 8, "episode_name": "Ep 8: Charlie Lozner | Climate Change and Personal Responsibility in the Outdoor Industry | Backbone Medi‪a‬", "slug": "ep-8-charlie-lozner-climate-change-and-personal-responsibility-in-the-outdoor-industry-backbone-media", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-8-charlie-lozner-climate-change-and-personal-responsibility-in-the-outdoor-industry-backbone-media", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "For Charlie, the work that gives him the most satisfaction is work that combats the effects of climate change. The outdoor industry has made strides in lobbying for Mother Earth but there is more work to be done.", "podcast_length": "44:30"}, {"id": "c117085d-6e00-4129-99bc-19f9d0a25324", "episode_id": "ee121690-42a3-4ed7-8bba-6415092b2a63", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c482a", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Charlie Lozner (15:06): Yeah, good question. I mean, I think the outdoor industry is waking up to it, and there have been brands that have been outspoken about it, but I think the industry as a whole has been a little bit slow to really bring that issue to the forefront on top of the outdoor industry is facing a ton of issues. Globalization, trade tariffs, the outdoor industry association was extremely focused on public lands for a long time and has made some incredible strides on that front get in the Land and Water Conservation fund passed last year. And a lot of other just big successes,\n\nSpeaker 3 (15:52): But\n\nCharlie Lozner (15:53): They're all sort of moot if we don't solve the climate change issue. And I think the outdoor industry association or just the brands that make up this industry, they're looked to as leading brands. And so there's an opportunity to do more.\n\nSpeaker 3 (16:09): And", "token_estimate": 228, "speakers": ["Charlie Lozner", "Speaker 3"], "start_timestamp": "15:06", "end_timestamp": "16:09", "podcast_index": 8, "episode_name": "Ep 8: Charlie Lozner | Climate Change and Personal Responsibility in the Outdoor Industry | Backbone Medi‪a‬", "slug": "ep-8-charlie-lozner-climate-change-and-personal-responsibility-in-the-outdoor-industry-backbone-media", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-8-charlie-lozner-climate-change-and-personal-responsibility-in-the-outdoor-industry-backbone-media", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "For Charlie, the work that gives him the most satisfaction is work that combats the effects of climate change. The outdoor industry has made strides in lobbying for Mother Earth but there is more work to be done.", "podcast_length": "44:30"}, {"id": "ca981d21-8910-4b05-bfa6-f28d1363a9eb", "episode_id": "ee121690-42a3-4ed7-8bba-6415092b2a63", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c482a", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Charlie Lozner (16:09): It's not just, there's a whole spectrum of things that brands can be doing from, I think more brands need to be thinking about offsetting, but that can be dangerous. Offsetting isn't a license to just get to spew more carbon into the atmosphere. You also have to think about how you're actually going to reduce your carbon impact. But then I think more brands need to think about what they can do just in Washington and with the political power that this industry has, it's almost a trillion dollar industry when you factor in the product sold the resorts, the travel, the hospitality, everything associated with that, everything that encompasses the outdoor industry, it's almost a trillion dollar industry. So it has weight if it organizes well and then steps on the gas in terms of political activism. And Aspen Ski Co has been extremely vocal on that front. Protect our winners has been extremely vocal on that front. So I think it's two pronged. It's like, yeah, you got to do the things that are right, figure out how do I offset, how do I reduce our own emissions? How do we improve our own operations? But then how can we take the position that we've got in the consumer mindset and the political weight that we've got as an industry and do more. (17:48): So it's super cool to see protect our winners like Jeremy Jones and Tommy Caldwell sitting in front of senators and talking about what they see when they're riding lines in Alaska or climbing in Yosemite and climbing in Yosemite is not the same now as it was 15 years ago. And that carries weight, and I think the outdoor industry could do a lot more of that.\n\nCole Heilborn (18:13): Yeah. Why do you think it's taken time for the outdoor industry to realize maybe how much power they have? Why has it taken time for them to start waking up and being vocal about this?", "token_estimate": 464, "speakers": ["Charlie Lozner", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "16:09", "end_timestamp": "18:13", "podcast_index": 8, "episode_name": "Ep 8: Charlie Lozner | Climate Change and Personal Responsibility in the Outdoor Industry | Backbone Medi‪a‬", "slug": "ep-8-charlie-lozner-climate-change-and-personal-responsibility-in-the-outdoor-industry-backbone-media", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-8-charlie-lozner-climate-change-and-personal-responsibility-in-the-outdoor-industry-backbone-media", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "For Charlie, the work that gives him the most satisfaction is work that combats the effects of climate change. The outdoor industry has made strides in lobbying for Mother Earth but there is more work to be done.", "podcast_length": "44:30"}, {"id": "cb098cb8-3f8f-418c-92d8-e761ad108f26", "episode_id": "ee121690-42a3-4ed7-8bba-6415092b2a63", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c482a", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Charlie Lozner (18:24): Totally, because we don't have an Exxon, we don't have a bp, it's a big industry, but it's a big industry built on the backs of passionate founder owners that a lot of the outdoor industry is tiny little like backbone. I mean, we're a tiny little company and there's a lot of 'em that make up the outdoor industry. I mean, the biggest companies in the outdoor industry are Patagonia, VF Corp, REI, but they're not, I mean, they're still a fraction of the size of an Exxon or bp, so it's hard to organize and mobilize an organization or a community that's built of so many tiny, tiny little companies. But that's the role that OIA can do that. SIA can do that Conservation alliance can do protect our winners, but it's about prioritization. And if I'm fighting about, I want e-bikes allowed in the wilderness and I'm fighting about, I want to protect this little tributary in my backyard and this other person's fighting about tariffs because tariffs are totally blowing up their business model because they outsourced everything to China, then the industry just struggles to organize. (19:53): But I think climate change is meaty. It's going to be incredibly hard to solve. The outdoor industry can play a role in that, but it's much bigger than that. Even the outdoor industry, I think sometimes talks out of both sides of its mouth talking about on one hand, oh, we need to knock down all these tariffs so we can build more stuff in China or Vietnam or countries with even worse records than those and ship it all around the world and air freight it when we're late and we don't get our orders in time, but oh, we have to save the climate too. We have to save the planet from climate change. So it may force some tough choices, and I think you're seeing it with COVID, you're with the tariffs. We may have to make more product closer to home, shorter supply chains, stuff like that, but it's going to be super tough to solve. But people are passionate about the brands in the outdoor space, and I think they'll listen when they speak up, whether that's politically or that's organizing, organizing voters and just encouraging folks to get out and vote in November, that's going to make the biggest impact.", "token_estimate": 558, "speakers": ["Charlie Lozner"], "start_timestamp": "18:24", "end_timestamp": "18:24", "podcast_index": 8, "episode_name": "Ep 8: Charlie Lozner | Climate Change and Personal Responsibility in the Outdoor Industry | Backbone Medi‪a‬", "slug": "ep-8-charlie-lozner-climate-change-and-personal-responsibility-in-the-outdoor-industry-backbone-media", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-8-charlie-lozner-climate-change-and-personal-responsibility-in-the-outdoor-industry-backbone-media", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "For Charlie, the work that gives him the most satisfaction is work that combats the effects of climate change. The outdoor industry has made strides in lobbying for Mother Earth but there is more work to be done.", "podcast_length": "44:30"}, {"id": "e5123904-0269-439a-b055-f316b15ef6b9", "episode_id": "ee121690-42a3-4ed7-8bba-6415092b2a63", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c482a", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Cole Heilborn (21:14): Yeah, yeah. That's interesting. It sounds like there's kind of two challenges there. There's one. First, the outdoor industry has to realize that they have the ability to make an impact if they can see that side of it or unify in some way, but then it's also Washington or whatever state you live in, that political system needs to recognize that the outdoor industry also has weight behind it. And I know locally here in Washington, the Washington I think has an outdoor industry of 22 billion, and it rivals the tech industry, which is phenomenal because you think of Seattle and you think of tech, but the outdoor industry is right there with it, if not leading it a little bit. And we're still having to be able to organize ourselves and show up in front of Olympia and say, Hey, we actually are this big industry. We have this economic success behind us. We are more scattered. And locally,\n\nCharlie Lozner (22:25): If you think of where the industry came from, I mean, it was hippies with backpacks and sandals like cruising around in the back country. The last thing they wanted to do was organize and mobilize. But the industry has successfully, and I think what the industry was capable of doing in the whole debate around public lands, which took years, decades to pass some of the legislation that has been passed, we can do it again. We've got to organize around that issue and reach beyond just the hikers and climbers and backpackers and skiers to the hunting community, the fishing community, maybe the RV community, maybe it sounds crazy, but maybe it's reaching into the moto sports community and convincing and promoting more of the, I mean, it's super cool to see the e-bikes coming along, and that's going to move into snowmobiles and four wheelers and side-by-side and stuff. So it's only going to work if we don't get super tribal.\n\nCole Heilborn (23:37): When you look at all this, what's your role in everything we've just been talking about? Where do you see yourself?", "token_estimate": 504, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Charlie Lozner"], "start_timestamp": "21:14", "end_timestamp": "23:37", "podcast_index": 8, "episode_name": "Ep 8: Charlie Lozner | Climate Change and Personal Responsibility in the Outdoor Industry | Backbone Medi‪a‬", "slug": "ep-8-charlie-lozner-climate-change-and-personal-responsibility-in-the-outdoor-industry-backbone-media", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-8-charlie-lozner-climate-change-and-personal-responsibility-in-the-outdoor-industry-backbone-media", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "For Charlie, the work that gives him the most satisfaction is work that combats the effects of climate change. The outdoor industry has made strides in lobbying for Mother Earth but there is more work to be done.", "podcast_length": "44:30"}, {"id": "4daae8d2-94f2-4f6a-a4b7-dcf452c2a895", "episode_id": "ee121690-42a3-4ed7-8bba-6415092b2a63", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c482a", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Charlie Lozner (23:45): I think it's not just me, it's backbone. We're connected to 60 or 70 brands. We have the ability to help craft how messages get out through those 60 or 70 brands. We also work with hundreds and hundreds of different media outlets, maybe thousands of different media outlets. We work with tons of different influencers for better or worse or whatever you want to think of, influencers. But yeah, it's everything from fashion influencers for some brands, but to the Tommy Caldwell, Alex Ho, Jeremy Jones, working with those types of influencers that have a voice. We did some work with the Sierra Club four years ago or five years ago where we helped Penn an op-ed that Alex Holl got in, I think it was in The Guardian about climate change and about, I think it was specifically about two coal factory or coal plants in Utah that had been just sort of given a haul pass to violate all sorts of EPA regulations and just spew, not just carbon, but pollutants into atmosphere. And then that all just cruises right over Colorado and onward. But yeah, so that type of work backbone just has an incredible network of clients, of media, of influencers, and I think it's the role we play is helping to get those important messages out. (25:33): It's not our job to create 'em. Our job isn't to necessarily build the campaigns or ideate the campaigns from the ground up, but we help with figuring out how to get those messages out.\n\nCole Heilborn (25:49): Yeah, that's awesome. Where does your passion come from?", "token_estimate": 382, "speakers": ["Charlie Lozner", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "23:45", "end_timestamp": "25:49", "podcast_index": 8, "episode_name": "Ep 8: Charlie Lozner | Climate Change and Personal Responsibility in the Outdoor Industry | Backbone Medi‪a‬", "slug": "ep-8-charlie-lozner-climate-change-and-personal-responsibility-in-the-outdoor-industry-backbone-media", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-8-charlie-lozner-climate-change-and-personal-responsibility-in-the-outdoor-industry-backbone-media", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "For Charlie, the work that gives him the most satisfaction is work that combats the effects of climate change. The outdoor industry has made strides in lobbying for Mother Earth but there is more work to be done.", "podcast_length": "44:30"}, {"id": "c4c82027-068f-4a16-aa3b-478d27886684", "episode_id": "ee121690-42a3-4ed7-8bba-6415092b2a63", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c482a", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Charlie Lozner (25:54): That's a good question. I think I'm just most passionate about getting outside and making sure that that opportunity exists for my kids, but really, yeah, that there's that opportunity for everyone. And that means that's products, that's access, that's everything from lower price points so that people can get into the sports easier. That's education. But yeah, am a lot of people in the outdoor industry, it just gravitated towards, I can't have a normal desk job where I tried it for four years and I couldn't do it. All I would do is all week to stream about getting out. And so the outdoor industry has allowed it to be a little bit blurry of what's work and what's play, and especially in Carbondale, but even in Seattle, you could sneak out and go for a ski tour and it was product testing or it was marketing, or it was a photo shoot or something like that. And so yeah, just a constant itch to go play and figure out how to show more people that that's, it's a great way to stay healthy physically, stay healthy mentally, learn about the environment and the world around you, and therefore care about it more.\n\nCole Heilborn (27:30): Yeah. One thing we haven't chatted about is your background. How did you end up where you are? Walk us through your journey.", "token_estimate": 323, "speakers": ["Charlie Lozner", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "25:54", "end_timestamp": "27:30", "podcast_index": 8, "episode_name": "Ep 8: Charlie Lozner | Climate Change and Personal Responsibility in the Outdoor Industry | Backbone Medi‪a‬", "slug": "ep-8-charlie-lozner-climate-change-and-personal-responsibility-in-the-outdoor-industry-backbone-media", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-8-charlie-lozner-climate-change-and-personal-responsibility-in-the-outdoor-industry-backbone-media", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "For Charlie, the work that gives him the most satisfaction is work that combats the effects of climate change. The outdoor industry has made strides in lobbying for Mother Earth but there is more work to be done.", "podcast_length": "44:30"}]
